55
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:41,470
And basically, it means
that from that root

56
00:02:41,470 --> 00:02:45,510
is going to blossom a whole
tree of possibilities.

57
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:49,030
And why could we argue that
is because mathematics use--

58
00:02:49,030 --> 00:02:52,610
requires reasoning and planning,
generalization and abstraction,

59
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:57,400
knowledge and creativity, open
ended and unbounded complexity.

60
00:02:57,400 --> 00:02:59,460
And over the years,
I also understood

61
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:03,180
that mathematics is not
just this application

62
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:05,110
of logical rules,
one after the other.

63
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:09,070
It's really a art.

64
00:03:09,070 --> 00:03:11,670
And so it even requires
an eye for beauty.

65
00:03:11,670 --> 00:03:13,510
And so I used a lot
of keywords here,

66
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:17,170
and that you'll see them
reappearing during my talk.

67
00:03:17,170 --> 00:03:21,270
And I hope that will make
this a bit more concrete.

68
00:03:21,270 --> 00:03:26,310
In summary, I've tried to
convey to you that mathematics

69
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:29,220
is a root node to understand
the natural world,

70
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:33,060
but it also might be a
root node for intelligence.

71
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:36,120
So I think it's worth really
thinking about mathematics

72
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:38,820
and see what can we do for that.

73
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:41,490
But before that,
I'd like to give you

74
00:03:41,490 --> 00:03:44,980
a really super brief history of
formalization in mathematics,

75
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,160
because basically
mathematics hasn't always

76
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:49,320
looked the way it looks now.

77
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,940
About 500 years before
Christ, the Greeks

78
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:55,720
had a great revelation.

79
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,900
They understood the
importance of the concept

80
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:00,280
of mathematical proof
and in some sense,

81
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:01,570
can you really imagine?

82
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:04,080
It's like you find
something that will tell you

83
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:07,120
with certainty that you
have attained eternal truth

84
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:10,330
and you can discover more and
more truths of our universe.

85
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:12,690
It must have felt incredible.

86
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:17,230
And since then, proofs is
at the heart of mathematics.

87
00:04:17,230 --> 00:04:19,209
And what constitutes
a proof, however,

88
00:04:19,209 --> 00:04:23,010
has evolved over the millenniums
of mathematical history.

89
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:25,740
One of the first breakthroughs,
I'm sure, that you know

90
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:29,650
is with Euclid and his book
Elements that had definitions,

91
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:31,210
axioms and theorems.

92
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:35,970
And so that's really the start
of axiomatization that has

93
00:04:35,970 --> 00:04:41,070
really been developed much more
in the early 20th century with

94
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:44,640
Hilbert Program that really
developed these to land on what

95
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:46,830
we have now.

96
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:49,420
And so that's
axiomatization on one side.

97
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:54,260
On the other side, like
mathematics, at the beginning

98
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:55,950
it didn't really use symbols.

99
00:04:55,950 --> 00:04:57,570
And that's for a very long time.

100
00:04:57,570 --> 00:05:00,230
Even if the Greeks had
taken find axiomatization

101
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:03,440
and started to use some
symbols, the rest of the world

102
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:06,920
describe really mathematics
with a lot of words.

103
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:09,320
And so here you have
a square and 10 times

104
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:11,400
its root is equal to 39.

105
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:15,230
So today we would write this as
x squared plus 10x equals 39.

106
00:05:15,230 --> 00:05:18,390
But look at this description of
how do you solve these things.

107
00:05:18,390 --> 00:05:20,900
Take the coefficient of the
root, divide it into two parts

108
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:22,620
and multiply one
of them by itself,

109
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:25,015
add these to 39,
cetera, et cetera.

110
00:05:25,015 --> 00:05:28,260
Now, you just write
this concise formula.

111
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:32,150
And there are two things
here that comes, it's first,

112
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:33,320
replacing--

113
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:37,470
using special-- in the
machine learning lingo,

114
00:05:37,470 --> 00:05:40,620
using special
tokens for numbers.

115
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:46,400
We represent-- the digits
have special characters.

116
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:48,182
But also even more important--

117
00:05:48,182 --> 00:05:49,890
I don't know which
one is more important,

118
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:52,060
but that helps with also--

119
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:54,580
I think only in
the 17th century,

120
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:59,860
René Descartes comes up with the
idea of using the early letter

121
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:02,890
of the alphabet to denote
constants and the end

122
00:06:02,890 --> 00:06:03,700
of the letter--

123
00:06:03,700 --> 00:06:07,210
of the alphabet x, y,
z to describe unknowns.

124
00:06:07,210 --> 00:06:10,040
And so that's basically how
you end up with this formula.

125
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:11,650
But it takes--

126
00:06:11,650 --> 00:06:14,680
I don't know how many-- it takes
until the 17th century to really

127
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:17,770
come up with those notations.

128
00:06:17,770 --> 00:06:19,840
And I think it's
not hard to argue

129
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:24,400
that precise notation and
axiomatization really eliminated

130
00:06:24,400 --> 00:06:28,060
ambiguities and logical error,
that symbolic notation made

131
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:30,160
complex manipulations
more tractable

132
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:32,900
and requires much
less cognitive load.

133
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:37,930
It also packs information
extremely densely

134
00:06:37,930 --> 00:06:39,170
in terms of notation.

135
00:06:39,170 --> 00:06:42,190
I can't remember exactly who
said that and if it was a joke

136
00:06:42,190 --> 00:06:45,580
or not, but someone was
arguing that maybe the Einstein

137
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:49,348
notations was his
biggest contribution.

138
00:06:49,348 --> 00:06:50,890
That might be pushing
it a little bit

139
00:06:50,890 --> 00:06:56,630
with given the impact of general
relativity, special relativity,

140
00:06:56,630 --> 00:06:59,380
and even his work to
introduce quantum mechanics.

141
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:04,487
But certainly, Einstein
notations are very important.

142
00:07:04,487 --> 00:07:06,320
Actually, if you read
machine learning code,

143
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:08,680
you'll see that
appearing a lot as well.

144
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:11,750
But basically, once
you have those symbols

145
00:07:11,750 --> 00:07:15,830
and you can lift yourself
up from particular examples,

146
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:19,330
you start to be able to observe
different common patterns

147
00:07:19,330 --> 00:07:20,810
across different contexts.

148
00:07:20,810 --> 00:07:23,680
And so that's where abstraction
and generalization comes up,

149
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,310
you can unify disparate
areas of mathematics.

150
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:29,990
And actually, you can
even create new fields.

151
00:07:29,990 --> 00:07:33,100
And so my favorite
example here is really

152
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,080
the people who are
looking at the 5

153
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:37,010
axioms of Euclidean geometry.

154
00:07:37,010 --> 00:07:39,830
And actually, I don't know,
for thousands of years,

155
00:07:39,830 --> 00:07:43,480
I think, trying to see if they
could remove the fifth axiom.

156
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:47,380
And that actually led to
non-Euclidean geometry.

157
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:49,980
And in particular
Riemannian geometry,

158
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:54,540
which in this theme
of general relativity

159
00:07:54,540 --> 00:07:57,810
was essential to get
general relativity.

160
00:07:57,810 --> 00:08:04,710
So this formalization has
helped mathematics, but also

161
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:08,490
the other sciences,
in particular physics.

162
00:08:08,490 --> 00:08:10,980
And so I would like to argue
that basically computer

163
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:13,890
formalization, which basically
means writing mathematics

164
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:17,580
in code, is just the next
logical phase in this grand arc

165
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:21,420
of mathematical formalism.

166
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:23,190
So there's been
quite a few languages

167
00:08:23,190 --> 00:08:25,500
to describe mathematical
reasoning, such as coq

168
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,800
and Isabellle, and Lean is
one of the most recent ones.

169
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:31,890
Originally developed, I believe,
for software verification,

170
00:08:31,890 --> 00:08:34,289
but also with
mathematics in mind.

171
00:08:34,289 --> 00:08:37,320
And mathematicians saw
in Lean the tool that

172
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:42,159
would make the next big step
in mathematical formalism.

173
00:08:42,159 --> 00:08:43,900
So it's a programming language.

174
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:46,380
It's a theorem prover and
interactive proof assistant.

175
00:08:46,380 --> 00:08:49,040
And Lean has a really
vibrant open-source community

176
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:51,020
of mathematicians,
and it's been used

177
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:54,290
to formalize like cutting-edge
mathematics, fields medal

178
00:08:54,290 --> 00:08:55,370
mathematics.

179
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:59,300
And then mathematicians
are on a huge quest

180
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:03,150
to basically build the
math library of the future.

181
00:09:03,150 --> 00:09:05,300
So let me go there.

182
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:06,690
It's called Mathlib.

183
00:09:06,690 --> 00:09:08,490
It's built open source.

184
00:09:08,490 --> 00:09:10,950
And basically, the free
time of mathematicians.

185
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:13,850
And it has two big goals.

186
00:09:13,850 --> 00:09:15,840
It aims to be
general and unified.

187
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:18,950
So general, it means that it
has the weakest assumptions

188
00:09:18,950 --> 00:09:20,790
and the strongest results.

189
00:09:20,790 --> 00:09:24,013
And unified is that it only
has one definition per concept.

190
00:09:24,013 --> 00:09:25,430
So I was actually
pretty surprised

191
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:28,760
when I learned about
this unification idea,

192
00:09:28,760 --> 00:09:31,790
because I thought
that mathematics

193
00:09:31,790 --> 00:09:36,300
was written in a very
structured way already.

194
00:09:36,300 --> 00:09:40,520
But apparently, it turns out
that, like for a given concept,

195
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:42,672
you may have several
papers that basically

196
00:09:42,672 --> 00:09:44,630
talk about the same
concept, but they define it

197
00:09:44,630 --> 00:09:45,690
in a different way.

198
00:09:45,690 --> 00:09:48,290
That is not necessarily
compatible with each other.

199
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:50,660
And so you need to do
quite a bit of work

200
00:09:50,660 --> 00:09:54,290
to actually try to find the
unifying definition that

201
00:09:54,290 --> 00:09:55,840
underlies all those ideas.

202
00:09:55,840 --> 00:09:57,590
And so that's actually
what takes the most

203
00:09:57,590 --> 00:10:01,310
time in building mastery.

204
00:10:01,310 --> 00:10:03,710
Maybe you can see,
in the presentation

205
00:10:03,710 --> 00:10:07,100
I gave here that it's one
monolithic library, where

206
00:10:07,100 --> 00:10:08,730
everything is
connected together.

207
00:10:08,730 --> 00:10:11,210
And so this is very
important for modern math

208
00:10:11,210 --> 00:10:14,150
because basically, modern
math is about trying

209
00:10:14,150 --> 00:10:15,643
to connect all areas of math.

210
00:10:15,643 --> 00:10:17,060
And there's been
observations that

211
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:21,780
actually like ideas from here
can help ideas from there.

212
00:10:21,780 --> 00:10:24,890
And for instance, maybe
Fermat's Last Theorem proof

213
00:10:24,890 --> 00:10:27,950
is a great example of this,
connecting two different areas

214
00:10:27,950 --> 00:10:31,250
to try to prove
one huge theorem.

215
00:10:31,250 --> 00:10:33,650
Now, there is also something
called the Langlands Program

216
00:10:33,650 --> 00:10:35,990
that really explicitly
tries to do that and connect

217
00:10:35,990 --> 00:10:36,750
all these areas.

218
00:10:36,750 --> 00:10:38,125
So it's really
important that you

219
00:10:38,125 --> 00:10:40,250
have a library that
basically can connect

220
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:42,800
all different areas of maths.

221
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:45,820
Currently, the library
covers about 80%

222
00:10:45,820 --> 00:10:50,150
of the undergrad curriculum, and
most of the library, however,

223
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:51,830
is above the undergraduate.

224
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:55,580
So it has a lot of
irregular coverage.

225
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:58,960
And because-- I think
particular examples are

226
00:10:58,960 --> 00:11:03,100
complex analysis or PDE theory.

227
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:04,000
OK.

228
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,050
All right.

229
00:11:05,050 --> 00:11:07,435
But why do all of this?

230
00:11:07,435 --> 00:11:10,150
Is because computer
formalization

231
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:12,670
unlocks enormous synergies.

232
00:11:12,670 --> 00:11:16,210
And maybe to explain myself a
little bit, I have to step back.

233
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:20,260
And for those of you who are
coders, maybe just understanding

234
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:22,910
the right code and be able to
use all the software stack,

235
00:11:22,910 --> 00:11:26,050
that should be already a
huge win for mathematics.

236
00:11:26,050 --> 00:11:27,880
But basically,
the other property

237
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:30,070
of when you write
mathematics in code

238
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:32,030
with those languages,
such as Lean,

239
00:11:32,030 --> 00:11:34,010
is that you get
perfect verification.

240
00:11:34,010 --> 00:11:37,530
So when you write a proof and
you write the correct proof,

241
00:11:37,530 --> 00:11:39,530
Lean is going to tell
you, yes, this is correct.

242
00:11:39,530 --> 00:11:41,030
And when you've
made a mistake, Lean

243
00:11:41,030 --> 00:11:43,810
is going to tell you this
is-- your proof is not correct

244
00:11:43,810 --> 00:11:45,790
or it's still incomplete.

245
00:11:45,790 --> 00:11:50,150
So from these two things,
you get some instant wins.

246
00:11:50,150 --> 00:11:54,220
First, your correctness
concerns completely disappear.

247
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:56,680
You can trust giant
proofs and you

248
00:11:56,680 --> 00:12:00,580
can delegate proof checking
entirely to the computer.

249
00:12:00,580 --> 00:12:03,340
And so I think it's
important to note

250
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:05,500
that it's my understanding
that it's not

251
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:09,940
like all areas of mathematics
are suffering from concerns

252
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:11,090
about correctness.

253
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:14,620
I think in reality,
most maybe areas,

254
00:12:14,620 --> 00:12:18,130
where mathematicians feel pretty
comfortable about their ability

255
00:12:18,130 --> 00:12:22,960
to discern correctness
from mistakes.

256
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:26,410
But I've been hearing that in
maybe some of the areas that

257
00:12:26,410 --> 00:12:28,580
are more abstract
areas, actually,

258
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:31,240
they start to be real
concerns for correctness.

259
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:34,840
So that's not everywhere, but
that starts to be important.

260
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:37,450
So these are the instant wins.

261
00:12:37,450 --> 00:12:41,550
But those perfect verification
and software stack

262
00:12:41,550 --> 00:12:44,190
has really a
knock-on effect that

263
00:12:44,190 --> 00:12:47,280
could lead to game changers.

264
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:50,740
So first, I think for all
of you who are coders,

265
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:53,700
to bring all the tools that
we have developed for coding

266
00:12:53,700 --> 00:12:56,490
should already be
a big, big win.

267
00:12:56,490 --> 00:13:00,850
Next, it have transformed
mathematics into a video game.

268
00:13:00,850 --> 00:13:02,950
So what does this
video game look like?

269
00:13:02,950 --> 00:13:04,660
Well, you have what
are the levels?

270
00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:06,840
The levels are the
different theorems.

271
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:10,960
And then you can
interact in those levels.

272
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:13,080
And basically, Lean will
tell you, yes, you've

273
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:16,800
completed the level or not
with this perfect verification

274
00:13:16,800 --> 00:13:17,670
feedback.

275
00:13:17,670 --> 00:13:19,710
You always have harder level.

276
00:13:19,710 --> 00:13:23,190
And maybe contrary to
other games, where--

277
00:13:23,190 --> 00:13:25,360
I don't know, you have
to shoot other people.

278
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:28,870
This is a super meaningful,
useful and profound game.

279
00:13:28,870 --> 00:13:32,250
You are basically adding
new truths for eternity.

280
00:13:32,250 --> 00:13:35,520
And the Lean people
describe this

281
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:39,150
as they could do
this 14 hours a day

282
00:13:39,150 --> 00:13:43,940
and they are constantly getting
positive reinforcements.

283
00:13:43,940 --> 00:13:48,140
This also means that it's great
for education because like a lot

284
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:50,810
younger people now are really
used to using computers

285
00:13:50,810 --> 00:13:51,980
and softwares.

286
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:55,160
And it's great because
it's interactive, right?

287
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:56,880
when it's fun,
it's easy to learn.

288
00:13:56,880 --> 00:14:00,110
And you can also
create software,

289
00:14:00,110 --> 00:14:03,230
such that the detail of--

290
00:14:03,230 --> 00:14:05,780
the level of detail is
adapted to the person who

291
00:14:05,780 --> 00:14:12,650
is reading rather than the level
that the author thought was

292
00:14:12,650 --> 00:14:13,380
interesting.

293
00:14:13,380 --> 00:14:15,540
I don't know if you read
a lot of maths papers

294
00:14:15,540 --> 00:14:18,620
or you look at solutions
of some problems

295
00:14:18,620 --> 00:14:19,620
you are trying to solve.

296
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:23,750
Sometimes people say this, well,
this next step is quite obvious,

297
00:14:23,750 --> 00:14:25,700
but maybe it is
not for you and you

298
00:14:25,700 --> 00:14:28,550
would love to see more detail.

299
00:14:28,550 --> 00:14:32,160
I have also an example
from-- actually from my dad,

300
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,760
who recently tried to
learn Galois theory

301
00:14:34,760 --> 00:14:37,850
in a different online
class, and so he

302
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:41,482
went through week 1 and
week 2 and week 3, all good.

303
00:14:41,482 --> 00:14:43,190
But then when he was
going through week 4

304
00:14:43,190 --> 00:14:44,565
and trying to do
the homework, he

305
00:14:44,565 --> 00:14:47,580
realized that there was
something he didn't understand.

306
00:14:47,580 --> 00:14:51,590
And he basically needed
someone to explain to him.

307
00:14:51,590 --> 00:14:54,980
And he messaged the professor,
but unfortunately, the professor

308
00:14:54,980 --> 00:14:56,100
didn't reply.

309
00:14:56,100 --> 00:14:58,400
And so he was basically
completely stuck.

310
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:01,530
But imagine that instead
he was doing this in Lean,

311
00:15:01,530 --> 00:15:03,105
then Lean would
have said exactly

312
00:15:03,105 --> 00:15:05,480
what is right, what is wrong,
what is missing, et cetera.

313
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:08,540
And he would be able to have
constant feedback without having

314
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:12,590
to ask his teacher.

315
00:15:12,590 --> 00:15:16,670
Now, I want to go
to the last point.

316
00:15:16,670 --> 00:15:20,040
It's actually a point that
has been made by Terry Tao,

317
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:25,610
I think, about a year
and a half ago, at least.

318
00:15:25,610 --> 00:15:30,020
And he describes that basically
using computer formalization

319
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:32,030
will unlock massive
collaboration

320
00:15:32,030 --> 00:15:33,480
between mathematicians.

321
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:34,680
And so why does he say that?

322
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:36,650
Well, apparently
the current state

323
00:15:36,650 --> 00:15:41,680
of mathematics collaboration
is that you can do--

324
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,950
most of the papers are either
single-author or two-author

325
00:15:44,950 --> 00:15:48,760
papers and the max is
really five-author papers.

326
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:52,900
And the reason why is
because every author needs

327
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:55,120
to check everyone else's work.

328
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:59,838
And so basically, having
more and more collaborators

329
00:15:59,838 --> 00:16:01,630
doesn't really scale
because you still need

330
00:16:01,630 --> 00:16:03,290
to check everyone else's work.

331
00:16:03,290 --> 00:16:05,890
And so that's where computer
formalization and [? password ?]

332
00:16:05,890 --> 00:16:10,120
verification comes in, is that
if you could trust the computer

333
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:13,270
to check the proofs,
then suddenly you can--

334
00:16:13,270 --> 00:16:17,047
it opens up working on
mathematical projects to, I

335
00:16:17,047 --> 00:16:19,630
don't know, thousands of people
because you could have someone

336
00:16:19,630 --> 00:16:22,100
you've never met, who lives
in the middle of nowhere,

337
00:16:22,100 --> 00:16:25,100
who contributes a little bit to
your proof of [? Lean ?] there.

338
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:27,140
And then Lean will say,
yes, this is correct.

339
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:28,730
And then you can
just move on, right?

340
00:16:28,730 --> 00:16:32,290
And so there's been
maybe-- there's

341
00:16:32,290 --> 00:16:33,740
been a few projects like this.

342
00:16:33,740 --> 00:16:37,363
I think Mathlib is maybe one
of the coolest demonstrations,

343
00:16:37,363 --> 00:16:39,780
where you have hundreds of
mathematicians working together

344
00:16:39,780 --> 00:16:42,040
to build this library
of the future.

345
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:43,890
But I think Terry
Tao is also arguing

346
00:16:43,890 --> 00:16:46,590
that you could use that
to do new mathematics,

347
00:16:46,590 --> 00:16:48,750
and has actually
launched a project

348
00:16:48,750 --> 00:16:51,420
in the past called
Universal Algebra, where

349
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:53,860
he was exploring that.

350
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:56,610
But basically, I think
in ML, at the moment,

351
00:16:56,610 --> 00:16:58,180
we are all about scaling.

352
00:16:58,180 --> 00:16:59,820
And here basically,
you could scale

353
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:03,030
humans to do mathematics
and potentially

354
00:17:03,030 --> 00:17:06,480
maybe tackle harder problems.

355
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:07,680
All right.

356
00:17:07,680 --> 00:17:11,200
So should everyone go to
computer formalization?

357
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:12,210
It's awesome.

358
00:17:12,210 --> 00:17:14,220
The reality is that there
are still challenges

359
00:17:14,220 --> 00:17:15,869
for computer formalization.

360
00:17:15,869 --> 00:17:19,980
In my estimates, about only
0.1% to 1% of mathematicians

361
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:21,210
have adopted Lean.

362
00:17:21,210 --> 00:17:25,230
It's a small community of
mathematicians, a vibrant one.

363
00:17:25,230 --> 00:17:29,520
But why is that? is because it's
actually not yet a great tool

364
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:31,170
for mainstream research.

365
00:17:31,170 --> 00:17:35,350
Like, a lot of
programming languages,

366
00:17:35,350 --> 00:17:38,490
it has a steep learning
curve, and it also

367
00:17:38,490 --> 00:17:40,590
requires a significant
time investment

368
00:17:40,590 --> 00:17:43,600
because to get
perfect verification,

369
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:48,480
you need to be able to
write every single detail.

370
00:17:48,480 --> 00:17:50,500
And so that takes
a lot more time.

371
00:17:50,500 --> 00:17:53,020
Maybe that's not the place
where you want to focus.

372
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:55,720
You want to focus on the things
you don't yet know how to solve,

373
00:17:55,720 --> 00:17:59,520
not on the things that
are more obvious to you.

374
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,050
And so that will be improved.

375
00:18:02,050 --> 00:18:05,850
And the tooling and
the library matures.

376
00:18:05,850 --> 00:18:09,270
But at the moment, there's
still some things missing.

377
00:18:09,270 --> 00:18:12,090
And obviously, there
is also a massive lack

378
00:18:12,090 --> 00:18:14,200
of necessity for research.

379
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:15,960
But I'm hoping
that the arguments

380
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:18,270
I've outlined in
the slide before

381
00:18:18,270 --> 00:18:22,080
are going to change a
little bit at that point.

382
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:26,220
So now I just want to show you
a little bit what it looks like.

383
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:33,120
So this is a proof that
you may know about.

384
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:35,780
It's called-- it's
about how many-- it's

385
00:18:35,780 --> 00:18:40,100
about proving that there is
an infinite number of primes.

386
00:18:40,100 --> 00:18:42,950
And OK, so this is what
it looks like in Lean.

387
00:18:42,950 --> 00:18:46,160
I hope you can see,
that it's big enough.

388
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:51,120
And so-- oops, OK.

389
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:51,940
Here it is.

390
00:18:51,940 --> 00:18:53,190
This is the theorem statement.

391
00:18:53,190 --> 00:18:56,090
And I think what
is remarkable is

392
00:18:56,090 --> 00:18:59,070
that because you
can use Lean code,

393
00:18:59,070 --> 00:19:01,790
this looks pretty much what
you would write yourself

394
00:19:01,790 --> 00:19:03,410
on a piece of paper.

395
00:19:03,410 --> 00:19:07,010
It says that for all n natural
numbers, there exists p,

396
00:19:07,010 --> 00:19:09,530
such that p is bigger
than n and p is prime.

397
00:19:09,530 --> 00:19:12,200
So that's how-- one way
to say that there is

398
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:15,470
an infinite number of primes.

399
00:19:15,470 --> 00:19:17,250
This is what the
proof looks like.

400
00:19:17,250 --> 00:19:20,150
So fairly compact in this case.

401
00:19:20,150 --> 00:19:22,610
And the fact that you can
understand all of this

402
00:19:22,610 --> 00:19:26,750
is because, basically Lean is a
language-based high level syntax

403
00:19:26,750 --> 00:19:29,630
mirroring normal mathematics.

404
00:19:29,630 --> 00:19:33,710
So let me go a
little bit on what

405
00:19:33,710 --> 00:19:35,780
it looks like to
interact with this thing.

406
00:19:35,780 --> 00:19:37,980
So here is the first
line of the proof.

407
00:19:37,980 --> 00:19:40,010
And this is actually
the main insight

408
00:19:40,010 --> 00:19:43,970
that Euclid had is to consider
first n factorial plus 1

409
00:19:43,970 --> 00:19:45,570
and the mean fact of it.

410
00:19:45,570 --> 00:19:46,700
So what is the mean fact?

411
00:19:46,700 --> 00:19:50,180
Is the mean factor of
n factorial plus 1.

412
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:51,830
And so I want to
draw your attention

413
00:19:51,830 --> 00:19:55,650
on the box on the right there
called the Tactic state.

414
00:19:55,650 --> 00:19:58,110
And so basically, that's
what Lean returns to you.

415
00:19:58,110 --> 00:20:00,022
It says, oh, here are
the things we know.

416
00:20:00,022 --> 00:20:01,980
And here are the things
we still need to prove.

417
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:04,230
So we know that n
is a natural number.

418
00:20:04,230 --> 00:20:08,418
We know that p is this mean
factor of n factorial plus 1.

419
00:20:08,418 --> 00:20:10,460
And you still need to show
that there exists a p,

420
00:20:10,460 --> 00:20:14,510
such that p is bigger
than n and p is prime.

421
00:20:14,510 --> 00:20:16,170
So let's go second line.

422
00:20:16,170 --> 00:20:20,090
Second line says, oh, let's
prove that n factorial plus 1

423
00:20:20,090 --> 00:20:21,270
is not one.

424
00:20:21,270 --> 00:20:24,740
And you have actually the proof
in line with maybe a few more

425
00:20:24,740 --> 00:20:28,760
characters than you really want
to prove something this easy.

426
00:20:28,760 --> 00:20:30,880
But on the right,
you see that you

427
00:20:30,880 --> 00:20:37,840
have a new fact in your arsenal
is that n factorial plus 1

428
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:39,670
is not one.

429
00:20:39,670 --> 00:20:43,810
And then we are going to say,
now let's prove that p is prime.

430
00:20:43,810 --> 00:20:46,960
And the proof you say, well,
it's because it's basically we

431
00:20:46,960 --> 00:20:48,770
took the minimum factor.

432
00:20:48,770 --> 00:20:52,360
The minimum factor
is obvious that it's

433
00:20:52,360 --> 00:20:56,180
a prime unless the thing
you've taken up is one.

434
00:20:56,180 --> 00:20:58,450
And so that's why
you have this thing

435
00:20:58,450 --> 00:20:59,810
you needed to prove before.

436
00:20:59,810 --> 00:21:02,200
And then the proof continues.

437
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:07,390
And at the end, you should see
in your Tactic state no goals.

438
00:21:07,390 --> 00:21:10,330
And it used to be, I think
in a previous version of Lean

439
00:21:10,330 --> 00:21:14,200
that you'd also received
this ta-da emoticon.

440
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:16,480
And I think
unfortunately, it's been

441
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:20,890
removed from the
current version of Lean.

442
00:21:20,890 --> 00:21:23,920
But basically, it's
your little reward

443
00:21:23,920 --> 00:21:27,130
for completing this level.

444
00:21:27,130 --> 00:21:31,720
So just to recap;
computer formalization.

445
00:21:31,720 --> 00:21:34,200
There is a really
vibrant community that

446
00:21:34,200 --> 00:21:36,780
has a huge enthusiasm for this.

447
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:40,740
They are building the library
of the future in their own time.

448
00:21:40,740 --> 00:21:44,130
It has many, many
ways in which it

449
00:21:44,130 --> 00:21:49,290
can unlock enormous synergies,
but it still has some way to go.

450
00:21:49,290 --> 00:21:53,790
And this community
and myself as well,

451
00:21:53,790 --> 00:21:55,980
we have the belief that
this will inevitably

452
00:21:55,980 --> 00:21:58,960
play an integral role in
the future of mathematics.

453
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:03,510
Just this work pushed the
computer formalization.

