495
00:24:13,430 --> 00:24:17,390
RL was used to try to
control in a token [? mac ?]

496
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:21,300
the shape of the plasma to get
better nuclear fusion reactions.

497
00:24:21,300 --> 00:24:26,420
So you can see, it is
a very general setup.

498
00:24:26,420 --> 00:24:30,770
Now, I had myself the
luck to spend my days

499
00:24:30,770 --> 00:24:33,920
on AlphaGo and its descendants.

500
00:24:33,920 --> 00:24:37,250
And this is what I
call the Zero Series.

501
00:24:37,250 --> 00:24:41,330
And especially from
AlphaGoZero, we

502
00:24:41,330 --> 00:24:44,240
showed that we could train
an agent to master domains.

503
00:24:44,240 --> 00:24:46,100
And when I say
master, I actually

504
00:24:46,100 --> 00:24:49,010
mean, being the world
champion, hands down

505
00:24:49,010 --> 00:24:52,910
at the time when
those things came out.

506
00:24:52,910 --> 00:24:55,760
And we learned to do that
without any human input

507
00:24:55,760 --> 00:25:00,020
information, or we basically
learned everything from scratch.

508
00:25:00,020 --> 00:25:03,920
And so I want to talk to you
about the Zero philosophy, which

509
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:07,760
I think is a great
philosophy for research.

510
00:25:07,760 --> 00:25:09,740
And it's about this.

511
00:25:09,740 --> 00:25:12,000
If an agent can learn to
master an environment,

512
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,293
tabula rasa, so without
any information,

513
00:25:14,293 --> 00:25:15,710
then basically
you've demonstrably

514
00:25:15,710 --> 00:25:17,390
shown that you
have a system that

515
00:25:17,390 --> 00:25:20,920
is able to discover and learn
new knowledge by itself.

516
00:25:20,920 --> 00:25:25,690
And it also means that this
algorithm is general and should

517
00:25:25,690 --> 00:25:28,810
apply to other domains.

518
00:25:28,810 --> 00:25:33,490
And I think the ability of being
able to discover new knowledge.

519
00:25:33,490 --> 00:25:36,460
This is particularly
important to advance science,

520
00:25:36,460 --> 00:25:38,230
because a big part
of doing science

521
00:25:38,230 --> 00:25:41,170
is to just explore areas that
are not yet well understood

522
00:25:41,170 --> 00:25:42,260
and advance knowledge.

523
00:25:42,260 --> 00:25:44,350
And you need to accumulate
knowledge, continuously

524
00:25:44,350 --> 00:25:46,790
generate and discover
new, interesting truths.

525
00:25:46,790 --> 00:25:50,980
And that's a huge
part of doing science.

526
00:25:50,980 --> 00:25:55,330
So especially, for research, I
think this is like a great way

527
00:25:55,330 --> 00:25:59,860
to demonstrate that you
can discover new things.

528
00:25:59,860 --> 00:26:04,870
I would say that, especially now
with the big success of LLMs,

529
00:26:04,870 --> 00:26:06,610
maybe a more pragmatic
point of view

530
00:26:06,610 --> 00:26:11,630
is to be able to start at
a certain knowledge base,

531
00:26:11,630 --> 00:26:14,140
and then you want to know
that your algorithm is

532
00:26:14,140 --> 00:26:16,430
going to continue to discover
new things from there.

533
00:26:16,430 --> 00:26:18,650
So you don't really
need to start from 0.

534
00:26:18,650 --> 00:26:21,150
What you really need is to
have this algorithm that

535
00:26:21,150 --> 00:26:22,390
can discover new things.

536
00:26:22,390 --> 00:26:27,345
And we've demonstrated
that you can do this

537
00:26:27,345 --> 00:26:29,860
with this research and
these AlphaZero [INAUDIBLE].

538
00:26:29,860 --> 00:26:32,650
So let me go into some examples.

539
00:26:32,650 --> 00:26:34,270
AlphaGoZero and AlphaZero.

540
00:26:34,270 --> 00:26:37,470
As I said, they learned simply
by playing against themselves,

541
00:26:37,470 --> 00:26:39,160
starting completely
from random playing.

542
00:26:39,160 --> 00:26:43,410
And what we observed
is that they basically

543
00:26:43,410 --> 00:26:46,050
rediscovered human
patterns, accumulating

544
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:47,800
thousands of years
of human knowledge,

545
00:26:47,800 --> 00:26:49,480
maybe in a matter of days.

546
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:52,260
But ultimately, some
of those patterns

547
00:26:52,260 --> 00:26:55,260
were discarded in preference
of new discoveries

548
00:26:55,260 --> 00:26:57,400
that humans don't
even know about,

549
00:26:57,400 --> 00:26:58,690
they don't even know about.

550
00:26:58,690 --> 00:27:03,510
So in Go, I believe that,
AlphaGo basically starting

551
00:27:03,510 --> 00:27:06,060
a new move at move--

552
00:27:06,060 --> 00:27:11,710
I would say move 5, a move that
wasn't even played by humans.

553
00:27:11,710 --> 00:27:14,430
And I've heard, if
I'm not mistaken,

554
00:27:14,430 --> 00:27:17,970
that Magnus Carlsen was saying
that because his coach was

555
00:27:17,970 --> 00:27:19,740
really good at
using engines and he

556
00:27:19,740 --> 00:27:22,990
got to use AlphaZero like
engines when those came out.

557
00:27:22,990 --> 00:27:26,640
He got a big advantage
momentarily over the other chess

558
00:27:26,640 --> 00:27:28,830
players, because basically
these things were playing

559
00:27:28,830 --> 00:27:32,220
very differently and
people were not used to it

560
00:27:32,220 --> 00:27:35,140
and they didn't know how to
handle those new situations.

561
00:27:35,140 --> 00:27:38,080
So that's Go and chess.

562
00:27:38,080 --> 00:27:40,470
And actually, if you look
at, for instance, the figure

563
00:27:40,470 --> 00:27:44,130
I showed on the
bottom right, and this

564
00:27:44,130 --> 00:27:46,860
is trying to show the
frequency of AlphaZero

565
00:27:46,860 --> 00:27:48,840
playing that particular
pattern called,

566
00:27:48,840 --> 00:27:50,530
for instance, the
Caro-Kann defense.

567
00:27:50,530 --> 00:27:53,430
And you can see that, it
basically discovers it

568
00:27:53,430 --> 00:27:54,970
after two hours of training.

569
00:27:54,970 --> 00:27:57,910
It plays it for about four
hours quite consistently.

570
00:27:57,910 --> 00:28:00,690
And then this pattern
starts to disappear

571
00:28:00,690 --> 00:28:02,500
from the games
AlphaZero is playing,

572
00:28:02,500 --> 00:28:07,770
probably because it has found a
way that makes this pattern not

573
00:28:07,770 --> 00:28:09,570
very interesting anymore.

574
00:28:09,570 --> 00:28:12,600
While on the bottom,
the Queen's gambit

575
00:28:12,600 --> 00:28:15,900
seems-- the frequency at which
the Queen's gambit is played

576
00:28:15,900 --> 00:28:20,810
is just increasing over
time-- over training time.

577
00:28:20,810 --> 00:28:21,740
All right.

578
00:28:21,740 --> 00:28:24,500
Now, let me talk
about Alpha Tensor.

579
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:26,750
And so this was a
work that was trying

580
00:28:26,750 --> 00:28:30,600
to find what is the best way to
multiply two matrices together.

581
00:28:30,600 --> 00:28:34,280
And so maybe the naive algorithm
that you may have learned

582
00:28:34,280 --> 00:28:38,390
at school requires n cube
operations when you are trying

583
00:28:38,390 --> 00:28:40,650
to multiply 2n by n matrices.

584
00:28:40,650 --> 00:28:43,640
So for 2 by 2 matrices, you
need eight multiplications,

585
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:48,660
for 4x4 multiplications,
you need 64 multiplications,

586
00:28:48,660 --> 00:28:50,150
et cetera, et cetera.

587
00:28:50,150 --> 00:28:52,640
And so there was at some
point a big discovery

588
00:28:52,640 --> 00:28:55,370
from a person named
Strassen that found that

589
00:28:55,370 --> 00:28:57,170
for 2 by 2 matrices,
actually you

590
00:28:57,170 --> 00:28:59,840
could do it with only
seven multiplications.

591
00:28:59,840 --> 00:29:02,520
And he also showed
that this was optimal.

592
00:29:02,520 --> 00:29:05,000
You couldn't do
better than that.

593
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:10,070
But as soon as we went to
4x4 matrices, basically,

594
00:29:10,070 --> 00:29:13,430
we knew nothing more than
applying Strassen algorithm

595
00:29:13,430 --> 00:29:14,360
twice.

596
00:29:14,360 --> 00:29:19,210
And so you still reduces from
64 to 49 multiplications.

597
00:29:19,210 --> 00:29:23,980
And so in AlphaTensor, we try
to discover a new efficient

598
00:29:23,980 --> 00:29:27,610
and provably correct algorithm
for basically tasks that look

599
00:29:27,610 --> 00:29:29,800
like matrix multiplication.

600
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:33,100
And it actually did.

601
00:29:33,100 --> 00:29:38,980
It found a new things in
actually many different sizes,

602
00:29:38,980 --> 00:29:43,420
discovering brand new knowledge
that humans never known about.

603
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:47,680
And so if you had only trained
on what humans had known,

604
00:29:47,680 --> 00:29:50,200
then you would have
never discovered those.

605
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:52,340
As a big bonus, as
part of this work,

606
00:29:52,340 --> 00:29:54,850
we show that basically
we could basically--

607
00:29:54,850 --> 00:29:57,700
AlphaZero could tackle
huge action spaces.

608
00:29:57,700 --> 00:30:00,050
So as I wrote here,
10 to the power 30.

609
00:30:00,050 --> 00:30:05,470
But basically for practical
purposes, this is just infinite.

610
00:30:05,470 --> 00:30:07,970
And so all those
successes Go, Chess,

611
00:30:07,970 --> 00:30:12,490
Tensor decomposition are all
specific to a certain task

612
00:30:12,490 --> 00:30:16,270
if you want a certain level,
but under the hood is basically

613
00:30:16,270 --> 00:30:18,820
the same AlphaZero algorithm
that is running and making

614
00:30:18,820 --> 00:30:20,350
those discoveries.

615
00:30:20,350 --> 00:30:22,040
And so what we had
in mind is like,

616
00:30:22,040 --> 00:30:23,740
is there an environment
that captures

617
00:30:23,740 --> 00:30:27,490
a much broader class of tasks
that are relevant for science?

618
00:30:27,490 --> 00:30:31,720
And you guessed it, our answer
is yes, it's mathematics.

619
00:30:31,720 --> 00:30:34,000
But just before we
get there, the lessons

620
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:37,360
we've learned from
all this work.

621
00:30:37,360 --> 00:30:40,720
So what made those
systems superhuman?

622
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:43,810
I've listed up what I
believe are the four

623
00:30:43,810 --> 00:30:45,620
most important properties.

624
00:30:45,620 --> 00:30:48,340
First is to scale
up trial and error.

625
00:30:48,340 --> 00:30:50,810
And basically to
discover new knowledge,

626
00:30:50,810 --> 00:30:52,630
you need to try
something new and be

627
00:30:52,630 --> 00:30:55,330
able to learn based on
the feedback you receive.

628
00:30:55,330 --> 00:30:57,370
And if your feedback
is not grounded,

629
00:30:57,370 --> 00:31:01,300
if you invent your own
feedback, then it's really--

630
00:31:01,300 --> 00:31:05,440
I think it's really hard once
you venture out of what's known.

631
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:07,450
And so you really
need a way to measure

632
00:31:07,450 --> 00:31:09,290
if you're achieving
your goals or not,

633
00:31:09,290 --> 00:31:13,330
where you are in the world--
where are you in your solution,

634
00:31:13,330 --> 00:31:14,970
et cetera, et cetera.

635
00:31:14,970 --> 00:31:18,430
And also AlphaZero
uses a lot of search,

636
00:31:18,430 --> 00:31:21,000
and I believe-- it's my
belief that search can really

637
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:23,880
accelerate the discovery
of new paths and skills.

638
00:31:23,880 --> 00:31:26,160
And it's because
most of the time when

639
00:31:26,160 --> 00:31:31,980
you sample a different
action, not the action that

640
00:31:31,980 --> 00:31:33,970
has the highest probability.

641
00:31:33,970 --> 00:31:37,170
If you try that action, most of
the time it's going to be bad,

642
00:31:37,170 --> 00:31:39,340
but sometimes it's
going to be good.

643
00:31:39,340 --> 00:31:42,360
And you need to discover
why that action might

644
00:31:42,360 --> 00:31:43,000
be good or not.

645
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:45,450
And so you need to
follow up on playing well

646
00:31:45,450 --> 00:31:47,190
after you play that action.

647
00:31:47,190 --> 00:31:51,930
And I think for instance,
AlphaGo and Move 37

648
00:31:51,930 --> 00:31:54,240
is a good example of that.

649
00:31:54,240 --> 00:31:56,890
It had a very low
probability of being played,

650
00:31:56,890 --> 00:31:58,570
but it was still
explored in the search.

651
00:31:58,570 --> 00:32:02,220
And then it was discovered
that was a really good move.

652
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:03,510
And so it became--

653
00:32:03,510 --> 00:32:06,480
it was played and
it was described

654
00:32:06,480 --> 00:32:08,790
as being very creative.

655
00:32:08,790 --> 00:32:12,450
And the final one is you need
to have a good curriculum

656
00:32:12,450 --> 00:32:14,400
to take you all the
way to superhuman.

657
00:32:14,400 --> 00:32:17,530
And in the AlphaZero case,
in Go and Chess and Shogi,

658
00:32:17,530 --> 00:32:20,440
we relied on the fact that
it was a two-player game.

659
00:32:20,440 --> 00:32:21,960
And so you could do self-play.

660
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:25,320
You basically always have the
right opponent to play against,

661
00:32:25,320 --> 00:32:28,330
and that was really important.

662
00:32:28,330 --> 00:32:31,860
So now let me try to
connect those two.

663
00:32:31,860 --> 00:32:35,070
Scaled up trial and
error; we have--

664
00:32:35,070 --> 00:32:38,400
Lean is the environment to
explore mathematics completely

665
00:32:38,400 --> 00:32:39,220
in silico.

666
00:32:39,220 --> 00:32:42,210
And the algorithm is going
to come from AlphaZero.

667
00:32:42,210 --> 00:32:45,680
And again, Lean is going to
give us this grounded feedback

668
00:32:45,680 --> 00:32:46,180
signal.

669
00:32:46,180 --> 00:32:48,670
It's going to tell us,
what have we proved so far,

670
00:32:48,670 --> 00:32:50,280
what's left to be proved.

671
00:32:50,280 --> 00:32:53,520
And it's going to tell
us when we are done,

672
00:32:53,520 --> 00:32:55,950
when we get a correct proof.

673
00:32:55,950 --> 00:33:00,670
For such, we can rely on
AlphaZero's search algorithm.

674
00:33:00,670 --> 00:33:01,860
It works pretty well.

675
00:33:01,860 --> 00:33:05,460
And maybe the question
mark is what do we

676
00:33:05,460 --> 00:33:07,980
do with the curriculum?

677
00:33:07,980 --> 00:33:12,290
And in some sense, where
are the levels coming from?

678
00:33:12,290 --> 00:33:14,810
So you've understood kind
of the idea of AlphaProof

679
00:33:14,810 --> 00:33:17,540
is to use Alpha
Zero for mathematics

680
00:33:17,540 --> 00:33:20,750
and in particular,
formal mathematics.

681
00:33:20,750 --> 00:33:27,320
So, let me make a napkin
master plan for AlphaProof.

682
00:33:27,320 --> 00:33:29,972
So Lean gives us a way to
scale up trial and error,

683
00:33:29,972 --> 00:33:32,180
with an environment to
explore mathematics completely

684
00:33:32,180 --> 00:33:35,930
in silico, and a perfect
feedback signal for proving.

685
00:33:35,930 --> 00:33:38,060
And then now, if we
extrapolate from the work

686
00:33:38,060 --> 00:33:41,120
we've done in the
other domains, and I

687
00:33:41,120 --> 00:33:43,580
conclude, we can therefore
reach superhuman intelligence

688
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:45,780
and discover new truths,
just like in Go, chess,

689
00:33:45,780 --> 00:33:49,790
Tensor decomposition provided we
can generate high enough quality

690
00:33:49,790 --> 00:33:53,120
and quantity of
problems and RL works.

691
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:56,340
So I'm very bullish on RL works.

692
00:33:56,340 --> 00:33:59,480
And so as I said, the
question that remains

693
00:33:59,480 --> 00:34:03,830
is how can we generate high
enough quality and quantity

694
00:34:03,830 --> 00:34:04,410
of problems?

695
00:34:04,410 --> 00:34:07,490
Where do the problems come from?

696
00:34:07,490 --> 00:34:10,000
And so I think one
of the answer has

697
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:15,100
to be that the problems
partly comes from the humans.

698
00:34:15,100 --> 00:34:16,960
And maybe the most
important reason

699
00:34:16,960 --> 00:34:22,120
why is that it's because we
want to help human mathematics.

700
00:34:22,120 --> 00:34:25,960
We could try to create
alien mathematics.

701
00:34:25,960 --> 00:34:30,010
But really, what we would like
is to help human mathematics

702
00:34:30,010 --> 00:34:33,130
and contribute in
that community.

703
00:34:33,130 --> 00:34:35,530
And maybe a more
pragmatic reason

704
00:34:35,530 --> 00:34:37,120
is that humans have
already come up

705
00:34:37,120 --> 00:34:41,255
with a lot of problems
that are quite interesting.

706
00:34:41,255 --> 00:34:42,880
But of course, we
should not throw away

707
00:34:42,880 --> 00:34:44,739
the second part,
where AlphaProof

708
00:34:44,739 --> 00:34:46,630
was defined the problem.

709
00:34:46,630 --> 00:34:50,380
And so I think there is
maybe two ways to slice this.

710
00:34:50,380 --> 00:34:53,260
One is AlphaProof
defines a problem

711
00:34:53,260 --> 00:34:54,710
around human mathematics.

712
00:34:54,710 --> 00:34:56,320
And we've done a
little bit of that

713
00:34:56,320 --> 00:34:58,672
already with auto
formalization and test-time RL.

714
00:34:58,672 --> 00:35:00,130
So it's perfectly
fine if you don't

715
00:35:00,130 --> 00:35:01,520
understand what I mean here.

716
00:35:01,520 --> 00:35:05,530
And I'll try to explain
those a bit later.

717
00:35:05,530 --> 00:35:08,110
But the other part is
basically really augmenting

718
00:35:08,110 --> 00:35:09,320
human mathematics.

719
00:35:09,320 --> 00:35:12,340
So ideally, AlphaProof
would be able to discover

720
00:35:12,340 --> 00:35:15,400
new areas of mathematics,
build new theories, et cetera,

721
00:35:15,400 --> 00:35:17,320
that humans find interesting.

722
00:35:17,320 --> 00:35:21,130
And yes, so these
are maybe where

723
00:35:21,130 --> 00:35:22,400
the problems can come from.

724
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:26,050
So before we move on, I
want to take a step back

725
00:35:26,050 --> 00:35:29,860
because if we're thinking
about AI for mathematics,

726
00:35:29,860 --> 00:35:32,260
there is potentially a choice.

727
00:35:32,260 --> 00:35:35,320
There is a choice of doing
it in natural language

728
00:35:35,320 --> 00:35:36,350
or in formal language.

729
00:35:36,350 --> 00:35:40,060
So I've been arguing a long
time now for the formal part.

730
00:35:40,060 --> 00:35:44,800
But actually, most effort
in AI, at the moment,

731
00:35:44,800 --> 00:35:47,980
is on informal mathematics or
doing basically mathematics

732
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:50,800
in natural language.

733
00:35:50,800 --> 00:35:53,710
And the reason why is
because basically all

734
00:35:53,710 --> 00:35:58,930
of the data of mathematics,
by many orders of magnitude

735
00:35:58,930 --> 00:36:01,970
is written in natural
language, of course.

736
00:36:01,970 --> 00:36:04,570
So you have this massive
library that is growing

737
00:36:04,570 --> 00:36:07,020
and that contains
quite a lot of maths,

738
00:36:07,020 --> 00:36:09,510
but it's still a few
orders of magnitude

739
00:36:09,510 --> 00:36:12,300
smaller than the
amount of mathematics

740
00:36:12,300 --> 00:36:17,740
you can find in textbooks, in
an archive and published papers,

741
00:36:17,740 --> 00:36:21,780
et cetera, that are all
written using natural language.

742
00:36:21,780 --> 00:36:23,970
So that's the trade
off you get when

743
00:36:23,970 --> 00:36:26,700
you use formal mathematics
is you really have

744
00:36:26,700 --> 00:36:28,510
a very small amount of data.

745
00:36:28,510 --> 00:36:30,870
That's when again, you
bump into the question

746
00:36:30,870 --> 00:36:34,380
of how do you get the problems,
where the problems come from.

747
00:36:34,380 --> 00:36:37,350
And however, I guess,
it's my opinion

748
00:36:37,350 --> 00:36:42,480
that because natural
language mathematics at least

749
00:36:42,480 --> 00:36:45,870
the reasoning part
is not verifiable.

750
00:36:45,870 --> 00:36:48,270
And I believe that this is
an issue if you're-- really

751
00:36:48,270 --> 00:36:51,420
your goal is to build a system
that can go beyond human

752
00:36:51,420 --> 00:36:53,520
knowledge and create
new knowledge.

753
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:56,520
And you can, of course,
improve the reliability

754
00:36:56,520 --> 00:36:58,350
of those systems.

755
00:36:58,350 --> 00:37:00,600
But the problem in
mathematics is really

756
00:37:00,600 --> 00:37:05,450
that if you build on
any false statement,

757
00:37:05,450 --> 00:37:08,220
then it destroys everything
you do afterwards.

758
00:37:08,220 --> 00:37:11,900
So you really need
like 100% reliability.

759
00:37:11,900 --> 00:37:15,950
And that's a property
of mathematics.

760
00:37:15,950 --> 00:37:21,240
And that's why I think that
verifiability is very important.

761
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:26,060
And basically, Lean is already
superhuman at verification.

762
00:37:26,060 --> 00:37:28,860
And I say all of
this for mathematics,

763
00:37:28,860 --> 00:37:31,530
but actually I believe this
is true for many important,

764
00:37:31,530 --> 00:37:34,700
impactful applications
that we cannot solve yet.

765
00:37:34,700 --> 00:37:38,060
And the scientific
problems, in particular.

766
00:37:38,060 --> 00:37:40,880
So in the domains,
we cannot solve--

767
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:42,480
there is almost
by construction--

768
00:37:42,480 --> 00:37:45,620
there is not much data
available because the domain is

769
00:37:45,620 --> 00:37:47,570
relatively poorly understood.

770
00:37:47,570 --> 00:37:50,240
And maybe it could even
be a completely new domain

771
00:37:50,240 --> 00:37:52,580
that has just been discovered.

772
00:37:52,580 --> 00:37:55,610
You could also have applications
that are somewhat niche.

773
00:37:55,610 --> 00:37:58,860
And maybe if you think
about what AGI would do,

774
00:37:58,860 --> 00:38:01,700
you could argue that maybe
mathematics is a bit niche,

775
00:38:01,700 --> 00:38:03,980
but it's still
extremely impactful.

776
00:38:03,980 --> 00:38:06,170
And it's conceivable
that generalization

777
00:38:06,170 --> 00:38:09,680
from all the other data
would be somewhat limited.

778
00:38:09,680 --> 00:38:12,200
And so if we had data
scarce, we really

779
00:38:12,200 --> 00:38:13,820
need a process that
can continuously

780
00:38:13,820 --> 00:38:16,460
generate and discover new
interesting facts that are not

781
00:38:16,460 --> 00:38:18,350
yet represented in the data.

782
00:38:18,350 --> 00:38:21,050
And you need a way to
grant those new facts

783
00:38:21,050 --> 00:38:23,660
so that you can check
that you are basically

784
00:38:23,660 --> 00:38:27,320
constantly adding new truths
and building on top of neutrals.

785
00:38:27,320 --> 00:38:29,030
And so this is really
a [? AlphaProof ?]

786
00:38:29,030 --> 00:38:32,160
foundational bet is that
perfect verification will,

787
00:38:32,160 --> 00:38:34,820
in the long run, be the
most important property

788
00:38:34,820 --> 00:38:37,160
for mathematics.

789
00:38:37,160 --> 00:38:38,450
So let me recap.

790
00:38:38,450 --> 00:38:40,070
RL meets formal math.

791
00:38:40,070 --> 00:38:42,890
So we have a proven recipe
to build an agent that

792
00:38:42,890 --> 00:38:44,690
can discover new
knowledge by themselves

793
00:38:44,690 --> 00:38:47,840
and reach superhuman
intelligence in certain domains.

794
00:38:47,840 --> 00:38:49,070
And the key ingredients--

795
00:38:49,070 --> 00:38:51,980
I have highlighted are
scaled up, trial and error

796
00:38:51,980 --> 00:38:53,280
and grounded feedback.

797
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:55,640
And basically, formal
math provides us

798
00:38:55,640 --> 00:38:57,920
with those key ingredients.

799
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:03,910
So these are the big ideas
that behind [INAUDIBLE].

800
00:39:03,910 --> 00:39:08,680
So now let me talk to you
a little bit about the IMO

801
00:39:08,680 --> 00:39:13,030
and our participation in 2024.

802
00:39:13,030 --> 00:39:15,250
So first, what's the IMO?

803
00:39:15,250 --> 00:39:17,530
The IMO stands for the
International Mathematics

804
00:39:17,530 --> 00:39:18,400
Olympiad.

805
00:39:18,400 --> 00:39:20,470
It's basically the
world-championship level event

806
00:39:20,470 --> 00:39:23,230
in mathematics that brings
together the best young minds

807
00:39:23,230 --> 00:39:25,240
from around the world.

808
00:39:25,240 --> 00:39:29,150
So every country brings their
top-six high school students,

809
00:39:29,150 --> 00:39:33,700
and they try to solve 6 problems
in two sessions of 4.5 hours.

810
00:39:33,700 --> 00:39:38,050
So basically in total, they have
nine hours to solve 6 problems.

811
00:39:38,050 --> 00:39:41,360
And in these six problems,
there are two easy problems,

812
00:39:41,360 --> 00:39:44,320
two medium problems
and two hard problems.

813
00:39:44,320 --> 00:39:46,030
So let me first
try to illustrate

814
00:39:46,030 --> 00:39:48,460
what it means to take
the top-six high school

815
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:50,650
students for the USA.

816
00:39:50,650 --> 00:39:55,930
So let's say there is about 17
million high-school students.

817
00:39:55,930 --> 00:40:00,220
It's not like every kid
loves maths and is going

818
00:40:00,220 --> 00:40:02,500
to do a maths competition.

819
00:40:02,500 --> 00:40:04,630
They might instead
play video games

820
00:40:04,630 --> 00:40:07,300
and maybe in the
future actually build

821
00:40:07,300 --> 00:40:09,080
a maths library of the future.

822
00:40:09,080 --> 00:40:11,680
But anyways, at the moment,
there is about 60,000 of them

823
00:40:11,680 --> 00:40:16,540
that participate in
these AMC competitions.

824
00:40:16,540 --> 00:40:21,100
If they do well and they
are still into this,

825
00:40:21,100 --> 00:40:25,900
they might go and participate
in this AMC competition.

826
00:40:25,900 --> 00:40:28,000
And so for those of you
who have been following

827
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:32,200
the benchmarks
used in AI, you may

828
00:40:32,200 --> 00:40:34,180
know about this
math benchmark that

829
00:40:34,180 --> 00:40:37,510
basically contains a lot of
AMC and [? AIME ?] problems.

830
00:40:37,510 --> 00:40:41,200
And so the reason why is
that, basically, AI need--

831
00:40:41,200 --> 00:40:44,035
what you need to do in
the [? AIME ?] is to--

832
00:40:44,035 --> 00:40:48,100
the solution is always an
integer between 1 and 1,000.

833
00:40:48,100 --> 00:40:53,140
And so it makes it easy to
check whether your system has

834
00:40:53,140 --> 00:40:54,800
outputted the correct
answer or not.

835
00:40:54,800 --> 00:40:56,260
Like, the correct number.

836
00:40:56,260 --> 00:40:59,160
You can check that
very easily and use

837
00:40:59,160 --> 00:41:01,523
that as a grounded feedback.

838
00:41:01,523 --> 00:41:02,940
The problem is it
doesn't tell you

839
00:41:02,940 --> 00:41:06,570
whether your reasoning to
get there is correct or not.

840
00:41:06,570 --> 00:41:13,740
Now, if you need to
get 177, then I still

841
00:41:13,740 --> 00:41:15,960
believe that it's indeed--
it's more likely that you

842
00:41:15,960 --> 00:41:19,600
have correct reasoning when you
come up with the right answer.

843
00:41:19,600 --> 00:41:22,167
And so that might still help
to improve the reasoning.

844
00:41:22,167 --> 00:41:24,000
And basically, what's
happening is improving

845
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:26,850
the reasoning of those models.

846
00:41:26,850 --> 00:41:29,190
But anyway, when you do
really well at the [? AIME, ?]

847
00:41:29,190 --> 00:41:33,600
you may participate in
the USA Maths Olympiad.

848
00:41:33,600 --> 00:41:35,820
There's only 250 now.

849
00:41:35,820 --> 00:41:37,660
Then if you are
in the top there,

850
00:41:37,660 --> 00:41:40,470
you may go to the IMO
camp and from there

851
00:41:40,470 --> 00:41:43,260
only the top six
people are selected.

852
00:41:43,260 --> 00:41:47,532
So you can see there is
basically six or seven orders

853
00:41:47,532 --> 00:41:48,990
of selection.

854
00:41:48,990 --> 00:41:52,380
And I'm told by my colleague
who did the IMO from--

855
00:41:52,380 --> 00:41:57,040
for the UK that they
study for 1,000 of hours,

856
00:41:57,040 --> 00:41:59,040
maybe an average of three
hours for three years.

857
00:41:59,040 --> 00:42:03,620
So really intense training
for this competition.

858
00:42:03,620 --> 00:42:07,520
Now, let me share a little
bit about the problems.

859
00:42:07,520 --> 00:42:10,560
And I'll start with this
quote from Po-Shen Loh.

860
00:42:10,560 --> 00:42:12,960
The Po-Shen Loh is not anyone.

861
00:42:12,960 --> 00:42:14,250
He's not new to the IMO.

862
00:42:14,250 --> 00:42:16,610
He was actually the
coach, the national coach

863
00:42:16,610 --> 00:42:18,950
for USA for the last 10 years.

864
00:42:18,950 --> 00:42:23,480
And he said, "I tried this
year's problems while at the IMO

865
00:42:23,480 --> 00:42:27,680
and it took me hours and context
about the IMO problems are

866
00:42:27,680 --> 00:42:30,260
specifically selected
to be non-standard."

867
00:42:30,260 --> 00:42:32,390
"So during the IMO
itself, national coaches

868
00:42:32,390 --> 00:42:34,620
meet to pick the problems
to appear on the exam.

869
00:42:34,620 --> 00:42:37,730
One of the most important
tasks of that group

870
00:42:37,730 --> 00:42:39,620
is to avoid problems
similar to problems

871
00:42:39,620 --> 00:42:41,010
that appear anywhere before.

872
00:42:41,010 --> 00:42:45,680
And so they go through a
great lengths to ensure this."

873
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:49,790
And so that's why basically
we really wanted to--

874
00:42:49,790 --> 00:42:52,610
we picked the IMO
as a goal for us

875
00:42:52,610 --> 00:42:55,430
is because we thought there
was really a test of reasoning

876
00:42:55,430 --> 00:42:58,260
and not of knowledge.

877
00:42:58,260 --> 00:43:02,070
And as Po-Shen Loh says,
even for specialists,

878
00:43:02,070 --> 00:43:04,340
even for the national
coach of the USA

879
00:43:04,340 --> 00:43:05,790
has been doing
this for 10 years,

880
00:43:05,790 --> 00:43:08,690
it would take him hours
to solve those problems.

881
00:43:08,690 --> 00:43:13,560
And the final property of
participating at the 2024 IMO

882
00:43:13,560 --> 00:43:16,080
was that those problems--
those problems were not leaked.

883
00:43:16,080 --> 00:43:22,100
And so you really need
it to be able to reason

884
00:43:22,100 --> 00:43:23,900
to solve those problems.

885
00:43:23,900 --> 00:43:27,440
And as I said, they are easy,
medium, and hard problems.

886
00:43:27,440 --> 00:43:30,770
Easy is in fact, those
problems are not easy

887
00:43:30,770 --> 00:43:36,110
at all because only
half of the participants

888
00:43:36,110 --> 00:43:38,390
solve two or more problems.

889
00:43:38,390 --> 00:43:42,800
And so you take the top six from
each country and half of those

890
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:46,270
won't be able to solve
the easy problem.

891
00:43:46,270 --> 00:43:46,770
All right.

892
00:43:46,770 --> 00:43:50,220
So now, let's go really
to this IMO 2024.

893
00:43:50,220 --> 00:43:54,850
And I'd like to relive
a part of it with you.

894
00:43:54,850 --> 00:43:58,180
So we had contacted
the IMO organization,

895
00:43:58,180 --> 00:44:03,110
and we had agreed with them that
they will send us the problems.

896
00:44:03,110 --> 00:44:07,750
We had contacted some judges,
also part of the competition,

897
00:44:07,750 --> 00:44:10,480
so they could grade
our solutions.

898
00:44:10,480 --> 00:44:14,690
And we try to participate.

899
00:44:14,690 --> 00:44:21,850
So I just want to make sure I
clarify exactly what our IMO

900
00:44:21,850 --> 00:44:23,390
participation was.

901
00:44:23,390 --> 00:44:25,390
And I think a good
way to think about it

902
00:44:25,390 --> 00:44:27,200
is more like an Apollo program.

903
00:44:27,200 --> 00:44:29,180
And so what we really
wanted to know is,

904
00:44:29,180 --> 00:44:31,010
can we actually reach the moon?

905
00:44:31,010 --> 00:44:35,140
Can our solar system solve
the 2024 IMO problem at all?

906
00:44:35,140 --> 00:44:37,750
And given the
computer that Google

907
00:44:37,750 --> 00:44:44,410
can give us at that time and
enough time for doing reasoning.

908
00:44:44,410 --> 00:44:46,900
So it was really
about this like,

909
00:44:46,900 --> 00:44:50,360
can we build a system that can
solve those problems at all?

910
00:44:50,360 --> 00:44:54,250
And I think at the time
when we got started,

911
00:44:54,250 --> 00:44:58,540
it was believed that this was
like five or 10 years away.

912
00:44:58,540 --> 00:45:03,640
So now let me take you to seven
months before January 2024.

913
00:45:03,640 --> 00:45:05,470
So we are seven
months away, and we

914
00:45:05,470 --> 00:45:07,310
need to make a decision
about geometry.

915
00:45:07,310 --> 00:45:08,890
So why geometry?

916
00:45:08,890 --> 00:45:12,940
Is because Mathlib is very
sparse in 2D Euclidean geometry,

917
00:45:12,940 --> 00:45:15,820
doesn't have much of what you
need to know about Euclidean

918
00:45:15,820 --> 00:45:19,630
geometry, mostly because
researchers don't really care

919
00:45:19,630 --> 00:45:22,660
about 2D Euclidean geometry
that is useful for the IMO.

920
00:45:22,660 --> 00:45:26,830
And so we're a bit
unsure what to do.

921
00:45:26,830 --> 00:45:29,020
We are debating whether
maybe we actually

922
00:45:29,020 --> 00:45:31,810
don't do geometry
at all or we try

923
00:45:31,810 --> 00:45:35,500
to fill in that part in
Mathlib so that our agent has

924
00:45:35,500 --> 00:45:39,910
the basic knowledge that is
expected every student to have.

925
00:45:39,910 --> 00:45:43,910
And so we are debating
this when miraculously,

926
00:45:43,910 --> 00:45:46,180
another group within
Google DeepMind

927
00:45:46,180 --> 00:45:49,000
turned out to have been working
on exactly the IMO geometry

928
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:49,700
problem.

929
00:45:49,700 --> 00:45:52,600
And they already have a system
that is very, very strong.

930
00:45:52,600 --> 00:45:54,900
Their system was
already approaching

931
00:45:54,900 --> 00:45:59,820
gold-level medal performance
on the geometric problems.

932
00:45:59,820 --> 00:46:03,630
We learned about that because
they published a paper in Nature

933
00:46:03,630 --> 00:46:05,470
in early January 2024.

934
00:46:05,470 --> 00:46:07,300
So miracle.

935
00:46:07,300 --> 00:46:10,420
But we decided to join
forces with them for July.

936
00:46:10,420 --> 00:46:12,420
And so AlphaGeometry
will tackle geometry

937
00:46:12,420 --> 00:46:15,150
and AlphaProof will tackle
algebra, number theory

938
00:46:15,150 --> 00:46:18,270
and combinatorics.

939
00:46:18,270 --> 00:46:20,620
A couple of months later, we
are debating another thing.

940
00:46:20,620 --> 00:46:24,100
So we've been training
AlphaProof on proving theorems.

941
00:46:24,100 --> 00:46:27,480
But not all questions are
on the form prove that.

942
00:46:27,480 --> 00:46:30,400
And actually some require
an answer to be determined.

943
00:46:30,400 --> 00:46:33,390
Like, find all x such that.

944
00:46:33,390 --> 00:46:37,600
And this is not just an
integer like the [? AIME, ?]

945
00:46:37,600 --> 00:46:42,390
It could be-- it's the
set of these numbers that

946
00:46:42,390 --> 00:46:44,280
have this property, et cetera.

947
00:46:44,280 --> 00:46:46,800
And so this actually
not obvious how

948
00:46:46,800 --> 00:46:49,520
to do well in formal
language because you have

949
00:46:49,520 --> 00:46:53,510
these tautological case where
it says, well, what are all

950
00:46:53,510 --> 00:46:56,160
the x such that property
p holds and says,

951
00:46:56,160 --> 00:46:59,450
what is- if the set of
numbers where p holds?

952
00:46:59,450 --> 00:47:02,100
And you have a really
easy time to prove that.

953
00:47:02,100 --> 00:47:07,820
But that's definitely not
what the judges were after.

954
00:47:07,820 --> 00:47:10,340
And so actually,
the answer itself

955
00:47:10,340 --> 00:47:12,890
is a bit of a subjective thing.

956
00:47:12,890 --> 00:47:15,860
And we debate what
we should do is

957
00:47:15,860 --> 00:47:20,150
we do what's known
outside as the easy mode.

958
00:47:20,150 --> 00:47:23,880
We basically feed in the
correct answer to AlphaProof,

959
00:47:23,880 --> 00:47:26,240
and we ask AlphaProof
to prove it.

960
00:47:26,240 --> 00:47:28,160
Or we go with hard
mode, where we

961
00:47:28,160 --> 00:47:30,800
try to determine the answer we
have-- something that determines

962
00:47:30,800 --> 00:47:33,350
the answer and we prove it.

963
00:47:33,350 --> 00:47:36,710
I personally don't really
like easy and hard keywords

964
00:47:36,710 --> 00:47:41,510
here because it's
fairly well known

965
00:47:41,510 --> 00:47:46,050
that the bit that is the hardest
for the IMO is the proving part.

966
00:47:46,050 --> 00:47:49,700
It's not really to
guess the answer.

967
00:47:49,700 --> 00:47:53,696
But anyway, so we are debating.

968
00:47:53,696 --> 00:47:55,470
We are not sure
exactly what to do,

969
00:47:55,470 --> 00:47:57,690
but after thinking
about it through,

970
00:47:57,690 --> 00:48:00,200
it's pretty clear that
we need to do hard mode.

971
00:48:00,200 --> 00:48:03,800
And what's cool is that
actually guessing the answer

972
00:48:03,800 --> 00:48:07,250
is exactly what Gemini
has been trained on.

973
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:09,830
And so we decided that we will
generate candidates ourselves

974
00:48:09,830 --> 00:48:12,620
with Gemini, and we will
attempt to prove and disprove

975
00:48:12,620 --> 00:48:15,770
all possible candidates
without proof.

976
00:48:15,770 --> 00:48:17,810
So that's how we're
going to participate.

977
00:48:17,810 --> 00:48:20,330
Now, the final thing
we are debating about

978
00:48:20,330 --> 00:48:24,505
is about the input that
the system should receive.

979
00:48:24,505 --> 00:48:26,630
So it will receive the
natural language description

980
00:48:26,630 --> 00:48:29,660
of the problem, for
instance, in English--

981
00:48:29,660 --> 00:48:32,120
actually, if you
participate at the IMO,

982
00:48:32,120 --> 00:48:33,620
you can choose the
language in which

983
00:48:33,620 --> 00:48:35,010
you will receive the problems.

984
00:48:35,010 --> 00:48:37,560
You're-- not everyone has
to read it in English.

985
00:48:37,560 --> 00:48:40,170
You can have it in Russian
or in Chinese or in Japanese.

986
00:48:40,170 --> 00:48:42,000
You can choose
your own language.

987
00:48:42,000 --> 00:48:45,820
Unfortunately, they don't
propose Lean as a language.

988
00:48:45,820 --> 00:48:50,080
And so we are debating
whether we will send English

989
00:48:50,080 --> 00:48:55,150
to the system or we'll
ask our experts internally

990
00:48:55,150 --> 00:48:57,130
to manually
formalize the problem

991
00:48:57,130 --> 00:49:01,090
and send that as an
input to the system.

992
00:49:01,090 --> 00:49:05,060
And so we debate this a
little bit with our judges.

993
00:49:05,060 --> 00:49:08,680
And very quickly, we
have the same opinion

994
00:49:08,680 --> 00:49:10,870
because what we
really care about is

995
00:49:10,870 --> 00:49:14,320
the mathematical reasoning and
problem solving at the IMO.

996
00:49:14,320 --> 00:49:17,120
And that's what
we are there for,

997
00:49:17,120 --> 00:49:20,470
not really about our
capabilities or actually

998
00:49:20,470 --> 00:49:24,310
translating the problem
in formal language.

999
00:49:24,310 --> 00:49:27,980
And so we decide that it's OK
for what we want to achieve,

1000
00:49:27,980 --> 00:49:31,240
that we will give the
formalized problems as input.

1001
00:49:31,240 --> 00:49:34,540
And so we ask our experts
to manually formalize

1002
00:49:34,540 --> 00:49:36,430
the problems for the system.

1003
00:49:36,430 --> 00:49:38,450
So if I recap.

1004
00:49:38,450 --> 00:49:41,000
Our protocol is going to
look something like this.

1005
00:49:41,000 --> 00:49:45,300
So we are going to officially
receive the problems at 1:00 PM.

1006
00:49:45,300 --> 00:49:49,680
the Lean experts will first
manually formalize the problems.

1007
00:49:49,680 --> 00:49:51,360
And then that's where the--

1008
00:49:51,360 --> 00:49:52,990
after that the AI comes in.

1009
00:49:52,990 --> 00:49:57,630
So we'll generate hundreds of
answer candidates with Gemini.

1010
00:49:57,630 --> 00:50:01,810
AlphaProof will try to filter
all the easily disprovable ones.

1011
00:50:01,810 --> 00:50:04,247
And then we are going
to run test time error.

1012
00:50:04,247 --> 00:50:06,330
So again, I haven't told
you what is test-time RL,

1013
00:50:06,330 --> 00:50:08,910
but I'm going to get there.

1014
00:50:08,910 --> 00:50:13,230
OK day one, we are there.

1015
00:50:13,230 --> 00:50:16,200
Tuesday at 1:00 PM, we
received the problem.

1016
00:50:16,200 --> 00:50:17,710
And P1 is an algebra.

1017
00:50:17,710 --> 00:50:20,710
P2 is a number theory and
P3 is a combinatorics.

1018
00:50:20,710 --> 00:50:25,680
And P1, P2 actually requires
an answer to be guessed.

1019
00:50:25,680 --> 00:50:30,580
And so we ask our experts to
manually formalize the problem,

1020
00:50:30,580 --> 00:50:33,490
then Gemini to guess
and second guess,

1021
00:50:33,490 --> 00:50:36,360
and then we get AlphaProof to
try to disprove all those answer

1022
00:50:36,360 --> 00:50:37,620
candidates.

1023
00:50:37,620 --> 00:50:42,090
And so we have already
very good signs

1024
00:50:42,090 --> 00:50:45,270
is that AlphaProof is able
to disprove 99% basically

1025
00:50:45,270 --> 00:50:48,600
of guesses for those two
problems in a matter of minutes.

1026
00:50:48,600 --> 00:50:50,170
And so this is a
really good sign,

1027
00:50:50,170 --> 00:50:51,587
because we've
observed in the past

1028
00:50:51,587 --> 00:50:54,130
that when AlphaProof
understands the problem,

1029
00:50:54,130 --> 00:50:55,650
it's going to be
able to disprove

1030
00:50:55,650 --> 00:50:57,580
a lot of false contexts.

1031
00:50:57,580 --> 00:51:00,450
But for problems that it
doesn't understand at all,

1032
00:51:00,450 --> 00:51:03,990
it wouldn't be able to
disprove much at all.

1033
00:51:03,990 --> 00:51:07,210
And so that's a really
good sign for day one.

1034
00:51:07,210 --> 00:51:11,350
On day two, we receive again,
at 1:00 PM the problems.

1035
00:51:11,350 --> 00:51:13,470
We knew actually
that P4 and P5 had

1036
00:51:13,470 --> 00:51:15,420
to be geometry and
combinatorics because there

1037
00:51:15,420 --> 00:51:18,990
is a rule that the easy
and medium problems--

1038
00:51:18,990 --> 00:51:22,730
all categories have to appear
in easy and medium problems.

1039
00:51:22,730 --> 00:51:25,440
But we are pretty
happy to see that P6

1040
00:51:25,440 --> 00:51:29,490
was algebra functional equation,
because we knew that also

1041
00:51:29,490 --> 00:51:33,930
AlphaProof was particularly
strong in functional equation

1042
00:51:33,930 --> 00:51:35,100
problems.

1043
00:51:35,100 --> 00:51:38,850
That said, P6 is in
the hard category.

1044
00:51:38,850 --> 00:51:40,930
And it's the hardest problem.

1045
00:51:40,930 --> 00:51:45,290
So even if it's a good
category for AlphaProof,

1046
00:51:45,290 --> 00:51:48,260
nothing is guaranteed at all.

1047
00:51:48,260 --> 00:51:51,140
And it turns out
that, AlphaGeometry

1048
00:51:51,140 --> 00:51:54,920
is able to solve the geometry
problem, I believe in seconds.

1049
00:51:54,920 --> 00:51:58,050
So really, really powerful.

1050
00:51:58,050 --> 00:51:58,950
It gets there.

1051
00:51:58,950 --> 00:52:01,970
So basically, they came
in, they run their thing

1052
00:52:01,970 --> 00:52:06,260
for a few seconds and
then it was done so then.

1053
00:52:06,260 --> 00:52:08,660
The P5 combinatorics
was actually very tricky

1054
00:52:08,660 --> 00:52:11,930
and our human experts failed
to formalize that problem

1055
00:52:11,930 --> 00:52:13,040
on the day.

1056
00:52:13,040 --> 00:52:16,790
And the P6 algebra,
it also requires

1057
00:52:16,790 --> 00:52:18,120
answers to be determined.

1058
00:52:18,120 --> 00:52:20,570
And Gemini generated
a bunch of answers.

1059
00:52:20,570 --> 00:52:23,100
And when we-- AlphaProof
try to disprove it,

1060
00:52:23,100 --> 00:52:25,850
it could only disprove
7% of guesses.

1061
00:52:25,850 --> 00:52:29,450
And so it doesn't have zero
traction on the problem,

1062
00:52:29,450 --> 00:52:33,710
but it doesn't have probably as
good an understanding as on P1

1063
00:52:33,710 --> 00:52:35,870
and P2.

1064
00:52:35,870 --> 00:52:37,110
All right.

1065
00:52:37,110 --> 00:52:43,200
So day 3, Thursday, AlphaProof
hasn't proved anything yet,

1066
00:52:43,200 --> 00:52:45,620
but we had a way
to track progress.

1067
00:52:45,620 --> 00:52:50,060
And so we observe on the
day 1 problems that P1--

1068
00:52:50,060 --> 00:52:52,850
AlphaProof had actually
solved half of the problem.

1069
00:52:52,850 --> 00:52:55,220
And so actually in
that proof, there

1070
00:52:55,220 --> 00:52:58,550
are at least two ways to
split that problem in two.

1071
00:52:58,550 --> 00:53:03,860
You'd case on the fact that
something is either even or odd

1072
00:53:03,860 --> 00:53:06,180
or that it's rational
or irrational.

1073
00:53:06,180 --> 00:53:11,330
And so AlphaProof had come up
with these ideas of splitting

1074
00:53:11,330 --> 00:53:12,930
this problem into 2 by itself.

1075
00:53:12,930 --> 00:53:15,210
And in, for instance,
in the even odd case,

1076
00:53:15,210 --> 00:53:18,020
it had proved the
even case and hadn't

1077
00:53:18,020 --> 00:53:20,540
finished proving the odd case.

1078
00:53:20,540 --> 00:53:23,330
It wasn't able to
prove the odd case yet.

1079
00:53:23,330 --> 00:53:28,070
In P2, when we look at it,
AlphaProof had arguably

1080
00:53:28,070 --> 00:53:29,750
solved the whole problem.

1081
00:53:29,750 --> 00:53:32,570
And so I don't know if
you know, but in the IMO,

1082
00:53:32,570 --> 00:53:35,960
there is a whole section where
after the competition is--

1083
00:53:35,960 --> 00:53:38,440
after the students have
submitted their answers,

1084
00:53:38,440 --> 00:53:45,250
the team captains can argue
with the judging committee that

1085
00:53:45,250 --> 00:53:48,640
actually like this student has
done this much and so therefore,

1086
00:53:48,640 --> 00:53:50,410
they deserve that much points.

1087
00:53:50,410 --> 00:53:53,270
And so if we had
followed that process,

1088
00:53:53,270 --> 00:53:56,060
we believe that with
whatever AlphaProof had,

1089
00:53:56,060 --> 00:53:58,880
we may be able to argue
for the whole problem.

1090
00:53:58,880 --> 00:54:01,810
So let's say, 6 out
of 7 points there.

1091
00:54:01,810 --> 00:54:05,680
And then on P3, the
combinatorics problem,

1092
00:54:05,680 --> 00:54:08,950
AlphaProof had hadn't
made any progress.

1093
00:54:08,950 --> 00:54:10,810
On the day two
problem, as I said,

1094
00:54:10,810 --> 00:54:14,870
Alpha Geometry hadalready
solved in seconds that problem.

1095
00:54:14,870 --> 00:54:16,430
So 7 out of 7 points.

1096
00:54:16,430 --> 00:54:21,170
The P5 combinatorics,
we had made no progress.

1097
00:54:21,170 --> 00:54:24,250
I think by that time, we
had a formalization of it.

1098
00:54:24,250 --> 00:54:28,840
But what was really cool is
that actually on P6 AlphaProof

1099
00:54:28,840 --> 00:54:33,430
had made progress and had proved
a particular case that we now

1100
00:54:33,430 --> 00:54:36,390
know is worth two
out of 7 points.

1101
00:54:36,390 --> 00:54:38,670
So probably on Thursday,
it had actually

1102
00:54:38,670 --> 00:54:41,380
made quite a bit of progress
on all the problems.

1103
00:54:41,380 --> 00:54:45,840
And we may have already
had like about 18 points.

1104
00:54:45,840 --> 00:54:51,180
Now, Friday we
received this email.

1105
00:54:51,180 --> 00:54:54,150
There is the name of my
colleague, Rishi, that

1106
00:54:54,150 --> 00:54:56,340
actually sent by AlphaProof.

1107
00:54:56,340 --> 00:54:58,380
And Alpha Proof is
sending an email

1108
00:54:58,380 --> 00:55:00,880
each time it solves one
of the IMO problems.

1109
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:04,560
It says I have discovered a
truly marvelous proof of IMO

1110
00:55:04,560 --> 00:55:06,220
2024 P6.

1111
00:55:06,220 --> 00:55:09,300
But this email is
too short to contain.

1112
00:55:09,300 --> 00:55:13,380
And of course, it's a
joke and the whole proof

1113
00:55:13,380 --> 00:55:15,040
is there in the email.

1114
00:55:15,040 --> 00:55:17,070
And so when we see
that, we are extremely

1115
00:55:17,070 --> 00:55:23,080
excited because we know we
have P2, AlphaGeometry has P4.

1116
00:55:23,080 --> 00:55:25,080
We're making progress on P1.

1117
00:55:25,080 --> 00:55:28,710
And we have seen that it looks
like we have solved the hardest

1118
00:55:28,710 --> 00:55:30,090
problem,

1119
00:55:30,090 --> 00:55:31,200
P6.

1120
00:55:31,200 --> 00:55:33,090
So extremely excited.

1121
00:55:33,090 --> 00:55:36,940
I run to my colleague's
desk, Oliver Nash,

1122
00:55:36,940 --> 00:55:38,440
who is one of our Lean experts.

1123
00:55:38,440 --> 00:55:42,760
And so we basically copy-paste
that proof into official Lean,

1124
00:55:42,760 --> 00:55:46,660
and it sinks a little bit and
says yeah, all good proofed.

1125
00:55:46,660 --> 00:55:49,320
And so I run back to my desk.

1126
00:55:49,320 --> 00:55:50,500
I don't think too much.

1127
00:55:50,500 --> 00:55:54,610
I just forward that message
to Demis and Sergey.

1128
00:55:54,610 --> 00:55:57,340
So it's like Demis
Hassabis and Sergey Brin.

1129
00:56:00,630 --> 00:56:05,410
And I wanted to share
that for two things.

1130
00:56:05,410 --> 00:56:09,060
One, I think it shows you
the trust I have in Lean.

1131
00:56:09,060 --> 00:56:12,150
If I didn't trust
Lean, I would probably

1132
00:56:12,150 --> 00:56:14,370
wouldn't have sent
that email seconds

1133
00:56:14,370 --> 00:56:18,480
after getting confirmation from
Lean that this was all good.

1134
00:56:18,480 --> 00:56:20,490
And the second thing
I thought a little bit

1135
00:56:20,490 --> 00:56:23,530
after that is like oh,
was this a stupid email?

1136
00:56:23,530 --> 00:56:25,890
But I think actually we
are in a company where

1137
00:56:25,890 --> 00:56:27,070
it's cool to do this.

1138
00:56:27,070 --> 00:56:28,547
And Demis and Sergey
were following

1139
00:56:28,547 --> 00:56:30,880
a little bit what was happening
during this competition.

1140
00:56:30,880 --> 00:56:34,460
So yeah.

1141
00:56:34,460 --> 00:56:37,340
A little bit later in
the day, actually, we

1142
00:56:37,340 --> 00:56:40,460
receive another mail and
it's a full proof of P2.

1143
00:56:40,460 --> 00:56:44,430
So now there is
nothing to argue about.

1144
00:56:44,430 --> 00:56:47,660
Perfect proof solved AlphaProof,
and a little bit later

1145
00:56:47,660 --> 00:56:49,700
in the day, actually,
on that same day we also

1146
00:56:49,700 --> 00:56:52,850
received a full proof of P1.

1147
00:56:52,850 --> 00:56:56,330
So in conclusion, final results.

1148
00:56:56,330 --> 00:56:59,460
P1, P2, and P6 were fully
solved by AlphaProof,

1149
00:56:59,460 --> 00:57:02,540
and P4 was fully solved
by AlphaGeometry.

1150
00:57:02,540 --> 00:57:04,970
And we actually try
to keep running over

1151
00:57:04,970 --> 00:57:07,380
the weekend in the hope of
getting one point on P3,

1152
00:57:07,380 --> 00:57:10,290
because actually the agent
made some progress on it,

1153
00:57:10,290 --> 00:57:15,650
but it turned out that it was
not enough for a partial point.

1154
00:57:15,650 --> 00:57:18,680
So we reached the score
of a silver medalist

1155
00:57:18,680 --> 00:57:23,660
and missed the gold
threshold by one point.

1156
00:57:23,660 --> 00:57:27,020
Of course, I just want to
reiterate that we definitely

1157
00:57:27,020 --> 00:57:29,360
use more time and
more compute than was

1158
00:57:29,360 --> 00:57:33,740
available to the
human participant.

1159
00:57:33,740 --> 00:57:36,120
I want to come back a little
bit on this P6 problem,

1160
00:57:36,120 --> 00:57:38,750
because it turned out that it
was arguably one of the hardest

1161
00:57:38,750 --> 00:57:41,270
problems in the last
years at the IMO.

1162
00:57:41,270 --> 00:57:45,410
Only five out of 609
of the participants

1163
00:57:45,410 --> 00:57:47,210
solved this problem fully.

1164
00:57:47,210 --> 00:57:50,510
And it's the top-six
people of every country

1165
00:57:50,510 --> 00:57:54,470
who comes in and only five in
total solve the problem fully.

1166
00:57:54,470 --> 00:57:59,510
And Sir Timothy Gowers, who is
Fields medalist and IMO gold

1167
00:57:59,510 --> 00:58:01,380
medalist as well,
he said that he

1168
00:58:01,380 --> 00:58:03,380
actually spent a couple
of hours on this problem

1169
00:58:03,380 --> 00:58:04,680
and did not solve it.

1170
00:58:04,680 --> 00:58:06,758
And I find the fact that
the program can come up

1171
00:58:06,758 --> 00:58:09,050
with a somewhat complicated
construction like this very

1172
00:58:09,050 --> 00:58:09,890
impressive.

1173
00:58:09,890 --> 00:58:16,220
So in no way am I saying that
we are close to Tim Gowers math

1174
00:58:16,220 --> 00:58:17,100
abilities.

1175
00:58:17,100 --> 00:58:19,970
And he may not have spent
these two hours really trying

1176
00:58:19,970 --> 00:58:22,490
to solve this,
and these things--

1177
00:58:22,490 --> 00:58:25,850
these are-- you need to
practice to solve these things.

1178
00:58:25,850 --> 00:58:28,550
And so in no way am
I saying that we are

1179
00:58:28,550 --> 00:58:30,210
close to Fields Medal level.

1180
00:58:30,210 --> 00:58:32,890
But I think we all
interpreted this

1181
00:58:32,890 --> 00:58:35,530
as a really, really
encouraging sign for the future

1182
00:58:35,530 --> 00:58:38,830
to be able to do something
really non-trivial.

1183
00:58:38,830 --> 00:58:42,490
And let me conclude
this IMO part with some

1184
00:58:42,490 --> 00:58:44,300
of the challenges we observed.

1185
00:58:44,300 --> 00:58:47,140
The first is the
gaps in Mathlib.

1186
00:58:47,140 --> 00:58:50,240
And so the most blatant
one is geometry, actually.

1187
00:58:50,240 --> 00:58:55,480
If AlphaGeometry
was not there, you

1188
00:58:55,480 --> 00:58:58,000
may have required us to
actually completely write

1189
00:58:58,000 --> 00:59:04,550
that part in Mathlib so that we
could do the geometry problems,

1190
00:59:04,550 --> 00:59:08,080
or we may have decided not even
to try the geometry problem.

1191
00:59:08,080 --> 00:59:11,720
And also, I want to
say that even in P1,

1192
00:59:11,720 --> 00:59:14,350
we observed that actually
there were some gaps in Mathlib

1193
00:59:14,350 --> 00:59:16,665
that we suffered from.

1194
00:59:16,665 --> 00:59:18,040
And so in one of
the proofs where

1195
00:59:18,040 --> 00:59:21,680
you have to prove that it's
either rational or irrational,

1196
00:59:21,680 --> 00:59:23,590
Alpha Proof hadproved
the rational part

1197
00:59:23,590 --> 00:59:26,420
and was struggling on
the irrational part.

1198
00:59:26,420 --> 00:59:31,000
And so this is not
definitive, but, in fact,

1199
00:59:31,000 --> 00:59:33,750
we observed that the
natural way to prove this

1200
00:59:33,750 --> 00:59:36,450
would be to use a certain
theorem that turned out

1201
00:59:36,450 --> 00:59:39,030
to not exist in Mathlib.

1202
00:59:39,030 --> 00:59:40,955
So this is not an excuse.

1203
00:59:40,955 --> 00:59:42,330
AlphaProof could
have conjectured

1204
00:59:42,330 --> 00:59:46,860
that theorem, proved it in line
and use it to finish that proof.

1205
00:59:46,860 --> 00:59:50,640
But it turned out that
problem was more difficult

1206
00:59:50,640 --> 00:59:52,890
than it needed to be.

1207
00:59:52,890 --> 00:59:56,730
Now, combinatorics problems
were also very difficult.

1208
00:59:56,730 --> 00:59:59,550
Just P5 was extremely
hard to formalize.

1209
00:59:59,550 --> 01:00:03,870
A lot of-- the necessary API to
represent those problems about

1210
01:00:03,870 --> 01:00:10,170
actually ironically about agents
environments and strategies is

1211
01:00:10,170 --> 01:00:12,060
not present in
Mathlib and that was--

1212
01:00:12,060 --> 01:00:14,370
made that problem very
hard to formalize.

1213
01:00:14,370 --> 01:00:17,350
And the reality is that even
when we had formalize it,

1214
01:00:17,350 --> 01:00:20,140
we did not manage to make
progress on P3 and P5.

1215
01:00:20,140 --> 01:00:24,600
So combinatorics were
difficult for AlphaGo.

1216
01:00:24,600 --> 01:00:27,510
And of course, we use
many orders of magnitude

1217
01:00:27,510 --> 01:00:29,910
more compute than
human contestants.

1218
01:00:29,910 --> 01:00:30,960
In some way.

1219
01:00:30,960 --> 01:00:34,840
I think, we actually
successfully landed on the moon.

1220
01:00:34,840 --> 01:00:37,770
I think that is actually
the important part.

1221
01:00:37,770 --> 01:00:43,050
And we will want to be more
efficient for the next trip.

1222
01:00:43,050 --> 01:00:44,610
All right.

1223
01:00:44,610 --> 01:00:48,300
So now let me go
into the methods.

1224
01:00:48,300 --> 01:00:50,370
And before we get there,
I want to remind you

1225
01:00:50,370 --> 01:00:53,680
that, when we're talking
about the methods,

1226
01:00:53,680 --> 01:00:56,470
there are two important aspects
we needed to think about.

1227
01:00:56,470 --> 01:01:00,630
One is where do the
problems come from

1228
01:01:00,630 --> 01:01:03,480
and how are we going
to have an agent that

1229
01:01:03,480 --> 01:01:05,970
learns to solve those problems?

1230
01:01:05,970 --> 01:01:08,490
So we trained several models.

1231
01:01:08,490 --> 01:01:10,720
One model is a formalized model.

1232
01:01:10,720 --> 01:01:12,810
So it takes as an
input a problem

1233
01:01:12,810 --> 01:01:16,600
described in natural language
in output a Lean formalization.

1234
01:01:16,600 --> 01:01:18,630
So this is based
on the idea that we

1235
01:01:18,630 --> 01:01:22,320
are going to lean onto
human-defined problems

1236
01:01:22,320 --> 01:01:24,520
instead of inventing
our own problems,

1237
01:01:24,520 --> 01:01:26,540
and we are going to
try to translate them

1238
01:01:26,540 --> 01:01:28,980
in Lean, because they all
exist in natural language,

1239
01:01:28,980 --> 01:01:30,320
but they don't exist in it.

1240
01:01:30,320 --> 01:01:33,130
So we're going to
try to do that--

1241
01:01:33,130 --> 01:01:36,350
trying to do that with
humans don't scale at all,

1242
01:01:36,350 --> 01:01:37,440
takes a long time.

1243
01:01:37,440 --> 01:01:40,520
And so instead, we want to
try to train a model that

1244
01:01:40,520 --> 01:01:43,620
can do this automatically.

1245
01:01:43,620 --> 01:01:45,810
That's the formalized model.

1246
01:01:45,810 --> 01:01:47,490
And now we also
have a proven model.

1247
01:01:47,490 --> 01:01:51,290
So the architecture of a proven
model is that it takes this--

1248
01:01:51,290 --> 01:01:53,750
if you remember in
my demonstration

1249
01:01:53,750 --> 01:01:57,180
before this yellow
box, the Tactic state,

1250
01:01:57,180 --> 01:01:59,790
the Lean state that tells
you, what do you know now?

1251
01:01:59,790 --> 01:02:00,900
What do you need to prove?

1252
01:02:00,900 --> 01:02:03,590
This is the thing we're
going to send as an input

1253
01:02:03,590 --> 01:02:04,740
to the proven model.

1254
01:02:04,740 --> 01:02:08,780
And then the proven model is
going to sample n continuation

1255
01:02:08,780 --> 01:02:10,190
for the proof.

1256
01:02:10,190 --> 01:02:15,590
This n-thing you might consider
potentially part of the proof--

1257
01:02:15,590 --> 01:02:17,330
an alternatives.

1258
01:02:17,330 --> 01:02:18,950
And then you take
this proven model

1259
01:02:18,950 --> 01:02:21,090
and you put it in
the AlphaZero search.

1260
01:02:21,090 --> 01:02:24,260
So you're going to search over
actions which are basically

1261
01:02:24,260 --> 01:02:25,640
one line in the proof.

1262
01:02:25,640 --> 01:02:27,530
Each time you add one
line in the proof,

1263
01:02:27,530 --> 01:02:28,700
you send that to Lean.

1264
01:02:28,700 --> 01:02:31,850
And Lean tells you,
oh, what do I know now?

1265
01:02:31,850 --> 01:02:35,290
What do I need to prove now?

1266
01:02:35,290 --> 01:02:38,350
And that's basically
how the search works.

1267
01:02:38,350 --> 01:02:41,810
If you know a little bit about
AlphaZero and MCTS like search,

1268
01:02:41,810 --> 01:02:44,900
they try to exploit what
the networks tell you--

1269
01:02:44,900 --> 01:02:46,220
the neural networks tell you.

1270
01:02:46,220 --> 01:02:49,210
They exploit the high-prior
and high-value paths.

1271
01:02:49,210 --> 01:02:53,020
But it has a mechanism also
to explore low-visited paths.

1272
01:02:53,020 --> 01:02:57,040
So, for instance, that's how
Move 37 in AlphaGo is discovered

1273
01:02:57,040 --> 01:02:59,570
is because it might
have a low prior,

1274
01:02:59,570 --> 01:03:02,960
it might not be considered
the right move by the network.

1275
01:03:02,960 --> 01:03:06,860
But it was still going to
try to visit it in case,

1276
01:03:06,860 --> 01:03:08,740
it might be an interesting move.

1277
01:03:08,740 --> 01:03:12,460
So now let me put this thing
together in the pipeline.

1278
01:03:12,460 --> 01:03:14,270
Step 1, we do auto
formalization.

1279
01:03:14,270 --> 01:03:16,900
So we train a formalization
model and auto formalize

1280
01:03:16,900 --> 01:03:20,110
human creative problems.

1281
01:03:20,110 --> 01:03:22,160
So this is really the
answer to the question,

1282
01:03:22,160 --> 01:03:25,430
where the problems come from
around human mathematics.

1283
01:03:25,430 --> 01:03:29,420
And here I want to highlight
a few advantages of using

1284
01:03:29,420 --> 01:03:31,650
formalization of the problem.

1285
01:03:31,650 --> 01:03:35,990
First is that when you send
a problem to Lean and Lean

1286
01:03:35,990 --> 01:03:39,050
says this is a valid
syntax, it means

1287
01:03:39,050 --> 01:03:41,480
that your question makes sense.

1288
01:03:41,480 --> 01:03:45,090
And it's not something
that doesn't make sense.

1289
01:03:45,090 --> 01:03:46,620
So it's either true or false.

1290
01:03:46,620 --> 01:03:50,570
You're generating something
that is either true or false.

1291
01:03:50,570 --> 01:03:52,890
OK, the problems might
not be interesting.

1292
01:03:52,890 --> 01:03:56,090
They might be trivially
true or trivially false.

1293
01:03:56,090 --> 01:03:59,040
But they are either
true or false.

1294
01:03:59,040 --> 01:04:00,710
And so one way to
see this is you

1295
01:04:00,710 --> 01:04:03,210
have the human
problem in the middle

1296
01:04:03,210 --> 01:04:06,895
And you try to generate
Lean variants of it.

1297
01:04:10,050 --> 01:04:11,780
If your model is
really good, then you

1298
01:04:11,780 --> 01:04:13,500
will do the perfect translation.

1299
01:04:13,500 --> 01:04:15,680
But maybe also it
will generate problems

1300
01:04:15,680 --> 01:04:20,140
that look like that problem.

1301
01:04:20,140 --> 01:04:22,660
And so when we have this
formalization model,

1302
01:04:22,660 --> 01:04:24,480
the way we use it
is that we were

1303
01:04:24,480 --> 01:04:26,890
able to gather about a
million informal problems.

1304
01:04:26,890 --> 01:04:29,280
So there is a lot
of problems to train

1305
01:04:29,280 --> 01:04:31,600
for the IMO, about
a million of them.

1306
01:04:31,600 --> 01:04:33,870
We pass them through
our formalized model

1307
01:04:33,870 --> 01:04:37,800
and we generate roughly
for every problem described

1308
01:04:37,800 --> 01:04:40,560
in natural language, we
generated roughly 100

1309
01:04:40,560 --> 01:04:43,560
possible translation for it.

1310
01:04:43,560 --> 01:04:46,560
And now we've just
created a huge database

1311
01:04:46,560 --> 01:04:49,620
of formal problems.

1312
01:04:49,620 --> 01:04:51,420
Now, we need to have--

1313
01:04:51,420 --> 01:04:54,930
we need to train the prover
to be good at proving.

1314
01:04:54,930 --> 01:04:57,450
And so that's why
you don't really

1315
01:04:57,450 --> 01:05:02,040
want to be completely in
the AlphaZero philosophy,

1316
01:05:02,040 --> 01:05:04,110
where you really
start from scratch.

1317
01:05:04,110 --> 01:05:07,115
Here, you want to
be pragmatic and you

1318
01:05:07,115 --> 01:05:08,490
want to start with
something that

1319
01:05:08,490 --> 01:05:11,350
is already can do a few things.

1320
01:05:11,350 --> 01:05:13,240
So you take a pretrained model.

1321
01:05:13,240 --> 01:05:15,990
In our case, it was
pretrained on a lot of code.

1322
01:05:15,990 --> 01:05:20,520
And then the next step is you're
going to train the approval

1323
01:05:20,520 --> 01:05:22,200
model supervisor Mathlib.

1324
01:05:22,200 --> 01:05:26,550
The Mathlib has about 100,000
definitions, 200,000 theorems,

1325
01:05:26,550 --> 01:05:28,590
300,000 lines of proof.

1326
01:05:28,590 --> 01:05:31,390
And basically, you are really
going to do supervised.

1327
01:05:31,390 --> 01:05:33,390
So, you are going to
place yourself inside

1328
01:05:33,390 --> 01:05:34,420
of a human proof.

1329
01:05:34,420 --> 01:05:36,700
You're going to look at what
is the mean state there.

1330
01:05:36,700 --> 01:05:38,880
Then you're going to look
at what the human said

1331
01:05:38,880 --> 01:05:41,250
was the next step in the
proof, and you're going

1332
01:05:41,250 --> 01:05:43,230
to train the model towards it.

1333
01:05:43,230 --> 01:05:47,820
So in some sense, very similar
to regular pretraining but you

1334
01:05:47,820 --> 01:05:49,830
get much more
information from it.

1335
01:05:49,830 --> 01:05:52,560
And so when you've done
that, your proven model

1336
01:05:52,560 --> 01:05:57,150
has learned a really good
prior over the actions to take.

1337
01:05:57,150 --> 01:06:00,840
And unfortunately,
Mathlib is about,

1338
01:06:00,840 --> 01:06:03,780
I don't know, but especially
if you want to do the IMO,

1339
01:06:03,780 --> 01:06:08,340
Mathlib doesn't contain
much IMO problems.

1340
01:06:08,340 --> 01:06:10,380
It's all about research math.

1341
01:06:10,380 --> 01:06:12,180
And so if you take
that proven model

1342
01:06:12,180 --> 01:06:19,460
and you try to apply it on the
IMO, you get around 0% of rate

1343
01:06:19,460 --> 01:06:20,660
on the end.

1344
01:06:20,660 --> 01:06:22,920
I'm not going to say 0%.

1345
01:06:22,920 --> 01:06:27,290
I actually am not sure,
but really close to 0%.

1346
01:06:27,290 --> 01:06:28,790
And so that's why
you start to have

1347
01:06:28,790 --> 01:06:33,020
to discover new knowledge about
how to solve those IMO problems.

1348
01:06:33,020 --> 01:06:37,430
And so that's where you enter
the next stage of the AlphaZero

1349
01:06:37,430 --> 01:06:39,030
reinforcement learning stage.

1350
01:06:39,030 --> 01:06:42,440
And so you are going to
train the proven model by RL.

1351
01:06:42,440 --> 01:06:44,490
And the recipe is very simple.

1352
01:06:44,490 --> 01:06:45,800
It's RL recipe.

1353
01:06:45,800 --> 01:06:47,990
And for each form
of problem, you

1354
01:06:47,990 --> 01:06:51,440
are going to generate experience
or either proving or disproving

1355
01:06:51,440 --> 01:06:54,410
that problem by
searching over steps.

1356
01:06:54,410 --> 01:06:58,200
And once you've come up with
what you think is a proof,

1357
01:06:58,200 --> 01:07:01,040
you're going to use Lean
to verify the proofs.

1358
01:07:01,040 --> 01:07:03,023
Lean is going to say,
this is not a proof.

1359
01:07:03,023 --> 01:07:03,690
This is a proof.

1360
01:07:03,690 --> 01:07:05,160
And for the things
that are proof,

1361
01:07:05,160 --> 01:07:10,040
you are going to reinforce your
proven network on those proofs.

1362
01:07:10,040 --> 01:07:13,160
And so what happens is
that, at the very beginning,

1363
01:07:13,160 --> 01:07:19,130
the proof model is able to prove
the very-- the easiest problems,

1364
01:07:19,130 --> 01:07:21,160
really, really easy
problems is going

1365
01:07:21,160 --> 01:07:24,700
to learn from that experience
and climb the next rung

1366
01:07:24,700 --> 01:07:27,530
on the ladder that leads all
the way to IMO strengths, right?

1367
01:07:27,530 --> 01:07:30,130
It's going to be able
to then solve a few more

1368
01:07:30,130 --> 01:07:31,520
problems a bit harder.

1369
01:07:31,520 --> 01:07:33,790
And by learning from
those, it will go and get

1370
01:07:33,790 --> 01:07:37,100
better and better and better
and all the way to the IMO

1371
01:07:37,100 --> 01:07:37,600
And.

1372
01:07:37,600 --> 01:07:42,670
So for this stage, we decided
to do a really the AlphaZero

1373
01:07:42,670 --> 01:07:47,728
pipeline, because what we wanted
to see is that, of course,

1374
01:07:47,728 --> 01:07:50,020
we want to solve the IMO,
but we want to do it in a way

1375
01:07:50,020 --> 01:07:53,930
that we think will generalize
way beyond the IMO.

1376
01:07:53,930 --> 01:07:59,110
So our real goal is to do
real mathematics is not

1377
01:07:59,110 --> 01:08:00,350
to stop at the IMO.

1378
01:08:00,350 --> 01:08:05,560
So we want to have a method
that is not tuned to the IMO,

1379
01:08:05,560 --> 01:08:08,480
and we want to have a method
that can discover new knowledge.

1380
01:08:08,480 --> 01:08:10,700
And so all these proving
strategies for the IMO,

1381
01:08:10,700 --> 01:08:15,370
this is completely discovered
by the model by itself.

1382
01:08:15,370 --> 01:08:16,700
So that's step 3.

1383
01:08:16,700 --> 01:08:20,680
And then final step is this
thing called test-time RL.

1384
01:08:20,680 --> 01:08:24,580
And so to give you a bit of
context, as I alluded before,

1385
01:08:24,580 --> 01:08:28,525
even for Po-Shen Loh, the USA
coach, or Tim Gowers or Terry

1386
01:08:28,525 --> 01:08:31,700
Tao, the problems at
the IMO are not obvious.

1387
01:08:31,700 --> 01:08:35,590
So even if you have a Fields
Medal level neural network

1388
01:08:35,590 --> 01:08:38,890
of mathematical intuition, you
cannot really solve those IMO

1389
01:08:38,890 --> 01:08:40,660
problems instantly.

1390
01:08:40,660 --> 01:08:43,630
But then-- even for them,
it takes hours to solve.

1391
01:08:43,630 --> 01:08:45,380
And why is that?

1392
01:08:45,380 --> 01:08:48,220
It's because, those problems
have been designed this way

1393
01:08:48,220 --> 01:08:51,740
and you really want to learn
something about those problems.

1394
01:08:51,740 --> 01:08:55,069
So it's almost a real
scientific experiment.

1395
01:08:55,069 --> 01:08:57,319
You first need to
think of experiments.

1396
01:08:57,319 --> 01:08:59,149
In this case, it's like
what could be true,

1397
01:08:59,149 --> 01:09:00,700
what could be false?

1398
01:09:00,700 --> 01:09:02,560
And then you need to
run the experiments

1399
01:09:02,560 --> 01:09:04,810
try to proves or
disproves those problems

1400
01:09:04,810 --> 01:09:07,330
and learn from this
experience to learn

1401
01:09:07,330 --> 01:09:09,609
about that particular problem.

1402
01:09:09,609 --> 01:09:15,149
And the way we did it
is that, AlphaProof

1403
01:09:15,149 --> 01:09:18,670
is going to generate variants of
the problem we are caring about.

1404
01:09:18,670 --> 01:09:21,100
So in this case, we have
these six problems at the IMO.

1405
01:09:21,100 --> 01:09:23,189
These are the problems
we care about.

1406
01:09:23,189 --> 01:09:25,410
Can we generate variants
of those problems

1407
01:09:25,410 --> 01:09:28,830
and basically then run RL
exactly as in the previous step

1408
01:09:28,830 --> 01:09:33,600
to try to discover knowledge,
especially about those problems?

1409
01:09:33,600 --> 01:09:38,910
So I'll try to illustrate this
in some hyperplane, where you

1410
01:09:38,910 --> 01:09:41,010
have the agent and the problem.

1411
01:09:41,010 --> 01:09:45,220
And so yeah, try to
imagine this a little bit.

1412
01:09:45,220 --> 01:09:49,470
So on the bottom left, basically
you have a bubble of problems

1413
01:09:49,470 --> 01:09:53,130
that the agent can
already solve easily.

1414
01:09:53,130 --> 01:09:55,000
And so it's a
generous checkpoint.

1415
01:09:55,000 --> 01:09:58,260
Every little black dots here
represent kind of a problem

1416
01:09:58,260 --> 01:10:01,110
that the agent already
knows how to do.

1417
01:10:01,110 --> 01:10:04,000
And then very far
on the top right,

1418
01:10:04,000 --> 01:10:06,520
you have this very hard
problem that you want to solve.

1419
01:10:06,520 --> 01:10:09,780
So imagine maybe it's the
P6 problem [INAUDIBLE].

1420
01:10:09,780 --> 01:10:13,835
And so as I described, the
first thing we did was we

1421
01:10:13,835 --> 01:10:15,210
generated variants
of that thing.

1422
01:10:15,210 --> 01:10:17,450
So we basically
explored that problem

1423
01:10:17,450 --> 01:10:19,280
into many, many variants.

1424
01:10:19,280 --> 01:10:22,940
And if you are
doing this well, you

1425
01:10:22,940 --> 01:10:26,360
may hope that it will generate
one variant that is actually

1426
01:10:26,360 --> 01:10:29,390
already solvable by the agent.

1427
01:10:29,390 --> 01:10:33,110
And so you solve that one
and you learn from that proof

1428
01:10:33,110 --> 01:10:34,410
exactly like before.

1429
01:10:34,410 --> 01:10:37,350
And you learned a little
bit about that problem.

1430
01:10:37,350 --> 01:10:38,820
And then you repeat
that process.

1431
01:10:38,820 --> 01:10:40,230
You repeat that process.

1432
01:10:40,230 --> 01:10:43,910
You learn about that
problem until you reach--

1433
01:10:43,910 --> 01:10:46,410
you reach that problem
and you can solve it.

1434
01:10:46,410 --> 01:10:50,060
And so that's how actually
P1 and P2 and P6, all of them

1435
01:10:50,060 --> 01:10:53,660
require test-time
RL to be solved.

1436
01:10:53,660 --> 01:10:57,530
So I want to finish this
section with some challenges

1437
01:10:57,530 --> 01:11:00,770
for AlphaProof.

1438
01:11:00,770 --> 01:11:04,650
So we inherit some challenges
from formal mathematics.

1439
01:11:04,650 --> 01:11:08,300
As I said, most of human
data is in natural language.

1440
01:11:08,300 --> 01:11:10,310
But we started to
have a good handle

1441
01:11:10,310 --> 01:11:12,740
on how to translate
that human data so

1442
01:11:12,740 --> 01:11:14,540
that we could learn about it.

1443
01:11:14,540 --> 01:11:17,930
And so there's still
a big challenge,

1444
01:11:17,930 --> 01:11:19,670
especially as we move forward.

1445
01:11:19,670 --> 01:11:24,890
But maybe we have some handle
on maybe something that is a bit

1446
01:11:24,890 --> 01:11:28,670
harder is that we cannot easily
learn and work on areas that are

1447
01:11:28,670 --> 01:11:30,680
not supported by Mathlib.

1448
01:11:30,680 --> 01:11:33,020
So for instance, like
in the geometric case,

1449
01:11:33,020 --> 01:11:35,270
where if you have
nothing about it, right,

1450
01:11:35,270 --> 01:11:39,380
you can't even express
the geometric problems

1451
01:11:39,380 --> 01:11:40,860
that humans have come up with.

1452
01:11:40,860 --> 01:11:46,220
And so that's a real
challenge to think about.

1453
01:11:46,220 --> 01:11:49,310
And so this could
be solved if you

1454
01:11:49,310 --> 01:11:52,400
start to understand how to do
creative building of new objects

1455
01:11:52,400 --> 01:11:53,780
and theories.

1456
01:11:53,780 --> 01:11:58,340
You understand interestingness
and beauty in mathematics.

1457
01:11:58,340 --> 01:12:05,570
But this is definitely a really
cool challenge for future work.

1458
01:12:05,570 --> 01:12:06,350
Cool.

1459
01:12:06,350 --> 01:12:09,480
So what's next for AlphaProof?

